window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("deployment", {"topics":[{"key":"deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","sources":["Official docs: Helm","Official docs: Kubernetes","Official docs: Sigstore (cosign)"],"why":"A hosted SaaS deployment assumes the customer will send you their data. For a bank's transaction\nrecords, a hospital's clinical notes, a defence contractor's documents or a government's citizen data,\nthat assumption fails before any technical discussion starts: the contract, the regulator or the\ncustomer's own classification policy says the data stays inside a perimeter they control. \"We encrypt\nit and promise not to train on it\" does not satisfy a rule about *where processing happens* and *who\ncan be compelled to hand it over*.\n\nThe naive answer is to copy your SaaS stack into their environment and run it as if it were yours.\nThat fails in three ways. First, your stack quietly depends on the open internet: container images\npulled from public registries, model weights fetched from a hub at start-up, Python packages installed\nat build time inside their network, licence checks and feature flags served from your SaaS, traces\nshipped to an observability vendor. Each of those is an outbound connection a security review will\nquestion and an air gap will simply break. Second, you no longer own the platform: their Kubernetes\nversion, their storage classes, their certificate authority, their egress proxy, their identity\nprovider. Third, you lose your eyes: no dashboards, no logs, no ability to hot-fix at 2 a.m.\n\nSo the pattern is not \"run the same thing elsewhere\". It is a different product shape: a signed,\nself-contained bundle, a configuration surface that captures every environment-specific decision,\nand an operating model in which the customer's platform team, not you, holds the keys. The further\ninward you go, the more of your product becomes packaging and the less of it is infrastructure you\noperate.","how":"### Three targets, one spectrum\n**Customer VPC (often sold as \"bring your own cloud\").** Your containers run in the customer's AWS,\nAzure or GCP account, in a VPC/VNet they own. Identity is their IAM; storage, databases and KMS keys are\ntheirs; model calls go to the cloud's managed model service (Bedrock, Azure OpenAI, Vertex AI) over a\nprivate endpoint, or to open-weight models on GPU instances in the same VPC. Your access, if any, is a\ncross-account role with a time-bound, logged session and an `ExternalId` condition to prevent the\nconfused-deputy problem. Many vendors split the system: a vendor-hosted **control plane** (licensing,\nversion catalogue, configuration, upgrade orchestration) and a customer-hosted **data plane** (every\ncomponent that touches customer data). The split is attractive *because* the customer's requirement\nis about data custody, not about metadata, so you keep operability for everything that is not data.\nIt must be optional *because* some customers reject any outbound connection on principle.\n\n**On-prem.** The customer's datacentre or colocation space. There are no managed services: you get a\nKubernetes distribution (OpenShift, Rancher, vanilla upstream, k3s) at whatever version they run,\ntheir storage classes, their ingress controller, GPUs exposed through a device plugin or the NVIDIA GPU\nOperator, an internal CA, and identity via their directory through OIDC or LDAP. Outbound traffic, if\nallowed at all, goes through a proxy that often terminates TLS with its own certificate. Your\ndeliverable is a Helm chart or operator plus images in a registry they can reach, usually a mirror\nthey populate from your registry.\n\n**Air-gapped.** Everything on-prem implies, plus: no network path exists. Artifacts cross the boundary\non physical media or through a one-way transfer device (a data diode or a cross-domain solution).\nInside, there is a mirrored container registry, a package mirror, their own time source and their own\nCA. Nothing can call home, so there is no licence server, no telemetry, no automatic update, and no\nhosted model. Updates are a new bundle carried in; support is a log bundle carried out.\n\n### The delivery pipeline\n1. **Build deterministically.** Pin every dependency, build images from a locked manifest, and make\n   sure nothing happens at container start that needs the network. Model weights are baked into an\n   image or seeded into a persistent volume from the bundle; `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` and\n   `TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1` turn Hugging Face libraries into pure local loaders; tokenizer files are\n   shipped, not fetched.\n2. **Sign and attest.** Sign images and the bundle manifest (cosign is the common choice), ship an SBOM\n   (SPDX or CycloneDX), and publish SHA-256 hashes. This is done *because* the receiving side cannot\n   ask you \"is this file right?\" and *because* the security review will ask how they know the\n   artifact is yours and unmodified. For an air gap, the simplest choice is a long-lived key pair whose\n   public key ships out of band. Cosign's default keyless mode ties signatures to a short-lived\n   certificate (Fulcio) and a transparency log (Rekor); it can be verified offline only if you also\n   ship the signature bundle with its log inclusion proof and the Sigstore trusted root, and keep that\n   root current, which is extra machinery many air-gapped sites decline.\n3. **Package.** Images exported with `docker save` or `skopeo copy` into a tarball or OCI layout, the\n   chart, the weights, a wheelhouse built with `pip download` for anything installed on-site, the\n   docs, and a verify script.\n4. **Transfer.** Registry-to-registry replication for VPC and connected on-prem; media or diode for\n   the air gap. A 70B-parameter model in 16-bit precision is roughly 140 GB of weights (2 bytes per\n   parameter), so the transfer itself is a planned activity, not a download.\n5. **Install.** `helm install` with a values file that holds every environment decision: registry\n   host, storage class, ingress class, CA bundle, proxy, model endpoint, identity provider, resource\n   limits. Hard-coded assumptions are the single biggest source of failed installs.\n6. **Verify.** Smoke tests that exercise the real path inside their network: a model call, a retrieval,\n   an authenticated request, a write to their database.\n7. **Operate.** Logs and metrics stay inside and flow to *their* backends (ship Prometheus and\n   OpenTelemetry collector configs that point at them). A `support-bundle` command gathers redacted\n   logs, config and version info into a tarball they can send you. Upgrade is a new bundle plus\n   `helm upgrade`; rollback is the previous chart version, which only works if your schema migrations\n   are backward compatible.\n\n### What changes about the software itself\nLicensing becomes a signed offline licence file with an expiry and a grace period, checked locally.\nFeature flags move from a remote service into the values file. Every HTTP client must trust the\ncustomer's CA bundle (`SSL_CERT_FILE`, `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`) *because* their proxy re-signs traffic.\nSecrets come from their secret store, not yours. Time-dependent behaviour (certificate expiry,\nlicence expiry, token lifetimes) must fail loudly and early *because* nobody will be watching a\ndashboard when it fails silently. And the model is whatever you shipped: on-prem and air-gapped\ncustomers run open-weight models on vLLM or similar, so your evaluation suite must have been run on\nthat exact model and prompt set before the bundle was cut.","example":"A minimal bundle manifest tool. `build` runs on your connected side after packaging; `verify` runs on\nthe customer's side, before anything is loaded into their mirror registry, so a corrupted 140 GB copy\nis caught before the install starts rather than during it. The manifest is then signed with\n`cosign sign-blob` (key-pair mode, so `cosign verify-blob --key` works offline) so the customer can\ncheck authorship as well as integrity. `verify` rejects three distinct failures: a file that is\nmissing (truncated transfer), a file whose hash differs (corruption or tampering), and a file that is\npresent but not in the manifest (something added after signing, which an installer that globs the\ndirectory would otherwise load). Hashing streams 1 MB blocks *because* a 140 GB weights file cannot be\nread into memory.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Build and verify an offline install bundle: `build <dir> <version>` or `verify <dir>`.\"\"\"\nimport hashlib, json, sys, pathlib\n\ndef sha256(path, chunk=1 << 20):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(chunk), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\ndef build(bundle_dir, version):\n    root = pathlib.Path(bundle_dir)\n    files = [p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file() and p.name != \"manifest.json\"]\n    manifest = {\"version\": version, \"artifacts\": {\n        str(p.relative_to(root)): {\"sha256\": sha256(p), \"bytes\": p.stat().st_size} for p in files}}\n    (root / \"manifest.json\").write_text(json.dumps(manifest, indent=1))\n    total = sum(a[\"bytes\"] for a in manifest[\"artifacts\"].values())\n    print(f\"{len(files)} artifacts, {total / 1e9:.1f} GB\")\n\ndef verify(bundle_dir):\n    root = pathlib.Path(bundle_dir)\n    manifest = json.loads((root / \"manifest.json\").read_text())\n    listed = manifest[\"artifacts\"]\n    present = {str(p.relative_to(root)) for p in root.rglob(\"*\")\n               if p.is_file() and p.name != \"manifest.json\"}\n    missing = [r for r in listed if r not in present]\n    altered = [r for r in listed if r in present and sha256(root / r) != listed[r][\"sha256\"]]\n    extra = sorted(present - set(listed))\n    for label, items in ((\"MISSING\", missing), (\"ALTERED\", altered), (\"UNLISTED\", extra)):\n        if items:\n            print(label, items)\n    ok = not (missing or altered or extra)\n    print(\"OK\" if ok else \"REJECT BUNDLE\")\n    return ok\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    cmd, path = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\n    if cmd == \"build\":\n        build(path, sys.argv[3])\n    else:\n        sys.exit(0 if verify(path) else 1)\n# Typical layout: images/app-1.8.3.tar, charts/assistant-1.8.3.tgz, models/<name>/, wheels/, docs/\n```","tradeoffs":["The customer keeps custody of data and uses their own cloud discounts and controls, but you lose direct observability and fast iteration; this flips toward hosted SaaS plus a strong DPA when the customer has no platform team to operate what you ship.","A control-plane/data-plane split keeps upgrades and configuration manageable across many customers, but any outbound connection is a review item; it flips to fully self-contained the moment the customer's policy forbids egress.","On-prem removes cloud dependency and per-token fees, but GPU capacity is fixed on a procurement cycle measured in months; it flips when the workload is bursty or needs frontier-model quality.","Air-gapped isolation is the strongest possible, but every bug fix is a physical delivery and bugs surface weeks later through log bundles; accept it only where mandated, and budget support time accordingly.","Shipping open-weight models makes the product deployable anywhere, but your quality bar must be re-established per shipped model, since the hosted model your SaaS uses is not an option inside."],"pitfalls":["Runtime downloads (model weights from a hub, tokenizer files, `pip install` on start, NLTK or tiktoken data): works in CI, then the pod crash-loops in the customer's cluster with DNS resolution or connection-timeout errors for a public hostname.","Hard-coded registry host, DNS domain or storage class in the chart: the symptom is `ImagePullBackOff` or persistent volume claims stuck in `Pending` on an otherwise healthy cluster.","Not trusting the customer's CA: every outbound call from your containers fails with a certificate verification error because their egress proxy re-signs TLS.","Licence or certificate expiry inside an air gap: the system stops on a date, nobody can renew online, and the customer discovers it during business hours.","Assuming you will have the logs: incidents are debugged from screenshots in email until a support bundle command exists and the customer knows how to run it.","Version sprawl across customers: each site is on a different bundle, so a fix must be back-ported to several versions; the symptom is a support matrix nobody can keep in their head.","Schema migrations that cannot roll back: a failed upgrade on-site leaves the customer unable to return to the previous chart version, turning a bad hour into a bad week."],"when":"Reach for a customer-VPC deployment when the blocker is data custody within a cloud the customer\nalready runs; it keeps managed model services and most of your operability. Go on-prem when the\ncustomer has no cloud footprint, needs GPU proximity to their systems or has a contractual\nprohibition on public cloud. Accept air-gapped only when a regulator or classification policy\nmandates it, and price the support model in. Before committing, measure what each tier costs you:\ninstall time per customer, bundle size, number of supported versions, and mean time to deliver a fix.\nIn day-to-day work this shows up as the values file, the bundle pipeline and the support-bundle tool.\nIn interviews, the expected depth is being able to list what breaks when the network goes away and\nhow the delivery pipeline changes as a result.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Removes every runtime network dependency, moves environment decisions into chart values, builds the signed bundle and the support-bundle command, and keeps migrations reversible.","architect":"Decides which deployment tiers the product supports, where the control-plane/data-plane boundary sits, and which models and dependencies each tier is allowed to assume.","fde":"Runs the install on the customer's cluster, negotiates registry, CA, proxy and identity details with their platform and security teams, and operates the sneakernet for air-gapped sites."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants your RAG assistant inside an air-gapped facility. Today the product calls a\nhosted frontier model, sends traces to a SaaS observability tool, downloads its embedding model from a\nhub at start-up and validates a licence against your server. What has to change, in what order, and\nwhat do you tell the customer they are giving up?","a":"First replace the hosted model with an open-weight model served inside (vLLM or similar) and\nre-run the evaluation suite on it, because that decides whether the product is still good enough to\nship. Then remove the start-up download by baking or seeding the embedding weights and setting the\noffline flags; replace the licence call with a signed offline licence file with expiry and grace; and\nredirect traces to a collector targeting the customer's own backend. Package images, chart, weights\nand wheels into a signed bundle with a hash manifest and a verify step, and add a support-bundle\ncommand. Tell the customer they lose frontier-model quality, automatic updates and vendor\nobservability; fixes arrive as bundles on a schedule, and they must operate the stack."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do many vendors split a customer-VPC deployment into a vendor-hosted control plane and a customer-hosted data plane?","options":["Because cloud providers require the control plane to run in the vendor's account","Because the customer's requirement is custody of data, so keeping only non-data functions vendor-side preserves operability while meeting the requirement","Because it removes the need for the customer to grant the vendor any IAM access","Because Kubernetes cannot run both planes in the same cluster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The split keeps licensing, configuration and upgrades manageable across customers while every component that touches data stays in the customer's account. It is a design choice, not a platform rule, and it must be optional because some customers forbid any outbound connection."},{"q":"An on-prem install crash-loops; the pod log shows a DNS resolution failure for a public model-hub hostname. What is the root cause and fix?","options":["The customer's cluster lacks a GPU operator, so the model cannot load","The Helm chart has the wrong storage class configured","The application downloads model or tokenizer files at start-up; weights must be bundled and the libraries run in offline mode","The customer's CA certificate is not trusted by the container"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A DNS failure for a hub hostname means code is trying to fetch artifacts at runtime. The fix is in the build: ship the weights and tokenizer files in the bundle and set the offline environment flags. A CA problem would show as a certificate verification error, not a resolution failure."},{"q":"Which artifact lets an air-gapped customer confirm a bundle is complete, unmodified and genuinely from you without contacting you?","options":["A README listing the included files and their versions","A licence file with an expiry date","A manifest of SHA-256 hashes signed with the vendor's key, plus signed images","A telemetry ping sent once the installation completes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hashes prove integrity and a signature over the manifest proves origin, and both can be checked offline. A README is unverifiable, a licence file proves entitlement rather than integrity, and a telemetry ping is impossible without a network path."}]},{"key":"deployment/private-endpoints","sources":["Official docs: AWS PrivateLink","Official docs: Azure Private Link","Official docs: Google Cloud Private Service Connect"],"why":"A bank's application subnet has no route to the internet: that is the point of the subnet. Now the\nteam wants to call a hosted model API, read documents from object storage and push traces to a SaaS\nobservability tool. Every one of those has a public hostname that resolves to a public IP.\n\nThe naive options all weaken the perimeter. Adding a NAT gateway gives the subnet outbound internet,\nso the same path that reaches the model API also reaches a paste site, which is exactly what the\ndata-loss team was preventing. Allowlisting the provider's IP ranges on a firewall is brittle, because\ncloud services use large, shared and changing address pools, so the allowlist ends up permitting a\nhuge slice of the cloud. VPC peering with the provider is usually not offered for multi-tenant\nservices, and where it is, it connects entire networks, requires non-overlapping CIDR ranges and\nexposes far more than one API.\n\nWhat the security team actually wants is narrower: \"this subnet may reach this one service, over a\npath we control, and that service refuses calls that did not come through that path.\" A private\nendpoint expresses exactly that. It also gives the service side a lever: once every legitimate\nconsumer arrives privately, the provider-side resource can disable public network access entirely,\nso a leaked API key is useless from the internet.","how":"The three large clouds implement the same idea under different names: AWS PrivateLink (interface VPC\nendpoints), Azure Private Link (private endpoints) and Google Cloud Private Service Connect. The\nmechanism has four parts.\n\n### 1. A network interface in the consumer's subnet\nCreating the endpoint places one or more network interfaces, each with a private IP from the\nconsumer's subnet, into the consumer's network. Packets sent to that IP are carried over the cloud\nprovider's internal network to the service. Because the consumer only ever sees an IP in its own\nrange, the two sides' address spaces never have to be compatible: overlapping CIDRs are fine,\nwhich is the main reason this beats peering for a SaaS that has thousands of customers who all use\n10.0.0.0/8. You place one interface per availability zone because the interface lives in a zone; a\nsingle-zone endpoint is a single point of failure and pushes cross-zone traffic.\n\n### 2. A service published on the provider side\nFor first-party services (model APIs, storage, key management) the cloud publishes the service for\nyou. For your own SaaS you publish it yourself: on AWS, an endpoint service fronted by a Network Load\nBalancer; on Azure, a Private Link service behind a Standard Load Balancer; on Google Cloud, a service\nattachment. You then decide who may connect, by allowlisting customer accounts or requiring manual\nacceptance of each connection request, because otherwise anyone who learns the service name could\nattach to it.\n\n### 3. DNS that points the normal hostname at the private IP\nApplications and SDKs use the service's ordinary hostname. If that name still resolves to a public IP,\nthe endpoint is never used. So the endpoint comes with a private DNS zone that overrides the public\nrecord inside the consumer network: the same hostname now resolves to the interface's private IP.\nKeeping the hostname unchanged is a deliberate design choice, because it means TLS certificates still\nvalidate and no SDK needs reconfiguring. For callers outside the VPC (on-prem over a VPN, or another\nnetwork) you must forward their DNS queries into the cloud resolver, otherwise they resolve publicly.\n\n### 4. Policy on both ends\nConnectivity is not authorization. Calls still need IAM credentials or API keys. On top, you can attach\nan endpoint policy (which principals and actions may use this endpoint) and, on the service, a\nresource policy or network rule saying \"only accept requests arriving via endpoint X\". The combination\nis what makes stolen credentials useless off-network.\n\n### Following one request\nPut the four parts together for a call from a pod to a model API:\n1. The SDK asks the VPC resolver for `bedrock-runtime.<region>.amazonaws.com`; the private zone\n   answers with the endpoint interfaces' private IPs (say 10.20.1.15 and 10.20.2.15, one per zone).\n2. The pod opens TCP 443 to 10.20.1.15. The **security group on the endpoint interface** must allow\n   443 from the pod's subnet; it is a separate control from the pod's own egress rules, and forgetting\n   it is the classic \"DNS is right but it still times out\" failure.\n3. The provider network carries the flow to the service's load balancer. The service never sees a\n   route into your VPC; it sees a connection tagged with the endpoint it came through, which is what\n   resource policies such as \"only via endpoint X\" match on. (For your own SaaS on AWS, enabling\n   Proxy Protocol v2 on the NLB is how your application learns which customer endpoint a connection\n   came from, *because* the source IP alone is not a reliable customer identifier.)\n4. TLS is negotiated against the ordinary hostname, so the provider's public certificate validates,\n   then the API key or IAM signature is checked as usual.\n\nNote the direction: connections are initiated by the consumer toward the service. The provider cannot\nopen connections back into the consumer network through the endpoint, which is why security teams\nfind it easier to approve than a VPN or peering link.\n\nOn AWS, gateway endpoints are a different, older mechanism available only for S3 and DynamoDB: they\nadd a route-table entry (a prefix list) instead of an interface, cost nothing extra, but only work from\ninside that VPC and not from on-prem or peered networks. Azure and Google Cloud have no direct equivalent;\ntheir closest analogues are service endpoints and Private Google Access respectively.","example":"A team runs a RAG service in a private subnet with no NAT. They created an interface endpoint for the\nmodel runtime API and one for object storage, then want a deploy-time check that the SDK's hostnames\nreally resolve privately. A failed check is the earliest signal that private DNS is off, which otherwise\nshows up only as timeouts (no egress) or, worse, silent internet traffic (if egress exists).\n\n```python\nimport ipaddress, socket, sys\n\n# Hostnames the application's SDKs actually call (region-specific in real deployments).\nREQUIRED_PRIVATE = [\n    \"bedrock-runtime.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\",\n    \"s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\",\n    \"vectors.internal.example.com\",   # our own SaaS exposed through a private endpoint\n]\n\ndef resolved_ips(host):\n    infos = socket.getaddrinfo(host, 443, proto=socket.IPPROTO_TCP)\n    return sorted({ipaddress.ip_address(info[4][0]) for info in infos})\n\nfailures = []\nfor host in REQUIRED_PRIVATE:\n    try:\n        ips = resolved_ips(host)\n    except socket.gaierror as e:\n        failures.append(f\"{host}: does not resolve ({e})\"); continue\n    public = [str(ip) for ip in ips if not ip.is_private]\n    if public:\n        failures.append(f\"{host}: resolves to public {public}; private DNS not active here\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"ok   {host} -> {[str(ip) for ip in ips]}\")\n\nif failures:\n    print(\"\\n\".join(\"FAIL \" + f for f in failures)); sys.exit(1)\n```\n\nRun it from inside the workload (a pod, a task), not from a laptop, because resolution depends on which\nresolver the caller uses. Storage services may answer with a gateway route rather than a private IP;\ncheck the route table for those.","tradeoffs":["You gain a narrow, one-directional path to exactly one service, but pay per-endpoint, per-zone hourly and per-GB charges; with dozens of services across many VPCs, a central shared-endpoint VPC becomes cheaper.","Disabling public access on the service blocks leaked credentials from the internet, but also blocks your own engineers' laptops and CI runners unless they reach it over VPN or a private runner.","Endpoints avoid CIDR coordination that peering or VPNs need, but they only carry traffic to the published service; if workloads need many services or bidirectional flows, peering or a transit network is simpler.","Private networking reassures a security team about the path, but it does not change who processes the data or where; if the concern is residency or training use, you need contractual and regional controls instead."],"pitfalls":["Creating the endpoint but not enabling or linking the private DNS zone; the symptom is the SDK timing out in a no-egress subnet, or flow logs showing traffic still leaving through the NAT gateway.","Forgetting on-prem and peered-VPC callers need DNS forwarding to the cloud resolver; the symptom is \"works from the cluster, fails from the data center\" with the hostname resolving to a public IP there.","Leaving the endpoint interface's security group at a default that does not admit 443 from the workload subnet; the symptom is that the hostname correctly resolves to a private IP but every connection times out, which teams misdiagnose as a DNS or provider outage.","Endpointing the main API but not its dependencies, such as the identity provider, token service or a separate file-upload host; the symptom is authentication or uploads hanging while plain calls succeed.","Deploying the interface in one availability zone; the symptom is a full outage of model calls during a zone incident and unexpected cross-zone data charges in normal operation.","Publishing your SaaS endpoint service with auto-accept for any principal; the symptom is unknown accounts appearing in the connection list, which an audit will flag immediately."],"when":"Reach for private endpoints whenever a customer's workload in a cloud network must call a managed\nservice and their policy says \"no internet egress\" or \"no public endpoints for data services\". That\ncovers most regulated buyers calling hosted model APIs, storage and key management in their own\naccount, and it is the standard way to offer your SaaS to enterprise customers without a VPN. Prefer\nit over VPN or peering when the flow is one-directional to a single service. Do not use it as a\nsubstitute for authorization, encryption or data-processing terms. Measure success by flow logs (zero\nbytes to the service via NAT) and by the service rejecting non-endpoint traffic. In interviews,\nexplaining the DNS override and the one-way property is what separates real experience from slideware.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the endpoint, security group and private DNS resources in Terraform, and adds a deploy check that every SDK hostname resolves to a private address from inside the workload.","architect":"Decides where endpoints live (per VPC or a shared hub), which services must disable public access, and how on-prem DNS forwarding is designed so hybrid callers stay private.","fde":"Walks the customer's network team through publishing or consuming the endpoint, requests account allowlisting, and debugs the inevitable DNS resolution difference between clusters and laptops."},"check":{"q":"A customer created a private endpoint for a hosted LLM API, yet their security team's flow logs\nstill show traffic from the RAG service going out through the NAT gateway. What is the likely cause, and\nhow do you make the setup fail closed?","a":"The endpoint exists but the application's hostname still resolves to the public IP, usually\nbecause private DNS was not enabled on the endpoint, the private zone is not associated with that VPC,\nor the workload uses a custom resolver that does not forward to the cloud resolver. Fix the resolution,\nverify with a lookup from inside the pod, then make it fail closed: remove the NAT route from that\nsubnet so a call that resolves publicly fails loudly, set the service's resource policy to accept only requests via\nthis endpoint, and turn off public network access where the service supports it. Finally add the DNS\ncheck to deployment so a regression is caught before traffic flows."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a SaaS vendor offer private endpoints to hundreds of customers who all use overlapping 10.0.0.0/16 ranges?","options":["The vendor runs a separate VPC per customer with a unique CIDR","Each customer reaches the service through an interface with an IP from their own subnet, so the two address spaces are never joined","Private endpoints use IPv6 internally, so IPv4 overlap is irrelevant","The cloud's NAT gateway rewrites addresses in both directions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The consumer only routes to a private IP in its own range and the provider network carries the traffic behind a load balancer, so no routes are exchanged. Peering and VPNs join address spaces and therefore require non-overlapping ranges."},{"q":"An endpoint for a model API is created and healthy, but calls from on-prem servers over the VPN still go to the public internet. What is missing?","options":["A second endpoint in another availability zone","An endpoint policy allowing the on-prem IP range","DNS forwarding so on-prem resolvers send the service's hostname to the cloud resolver that holds the private zone","A BGP route advertising the service's public IP range over the VPN"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The private zone only answers inside the cloud network. On-prem resolvers still return the public record unless queries for that domain are forwarded inward; advertising public ranges over the VPN would be a fragile hack, not the design."},{"q":"A security reviewer says \"we have a private endpoint, so stolen API keys are harmless.\" What makes that statement true or false?","options":["It is true by default, because private endpoints encrypt the API key","It is false, because private endpoints only work for storage services","It is true, because the endpoint policy replaces the need for credentials","It is only true if the service also rejects requests that did not arrive through the endpoint, for example by disabling public access"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The endpoint adds a private path but does not remove the public one. Only a resource-side restriction, such as disabling public access or a policy requiring this endpoint, makes the key useless from outside."}]},{"key":"deployment/vpn","sources":["RFC 7296 — Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)","RFC 4303 — IP Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)","Official docs: WireGuard"],"why":"Your RAG product runs in the cloud. The customer's contracts live in an on-prem document store, their\nticket history in a database in the same data center, and neither has, nor will ever have, a public IP.\nYour ingestion workers need to reach them on private addresses.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. Exposing the database or file share on the internet\nbehind a firewall rule gets rejected in the first security meeting, and rightly: credentials become the\nonly barrier. Shipping data on disks or nightly SFTP exports works once, but gives you stale data, a\nmanual process and no way to run live queries or tool calls. A dedicated private circuit (AWS Direct\nConnect, Azure ExpressRoute, Google Cloud Interconnect) is the right answer for heavy, permanent traffic,\nbut takes weeks to months to provision through a carrier and costs accordingly.\n\nA VPN is the middle path: it reuses the internet connection both sides already have, adds encryption and\nmutual authentication, and lets routers forward private-address traffic through the tunnel. It can be up\nin days, which matters during a pilot. Its weakness is the flip side of its strength: once two networks\nare joined, anything routable can talk to anything else unless you narrow it, so a VPN is a network\nintegration, not just an encryption feature.","how":"### Site-to-site IPsec\nMost enterprise site-to-site VPNs, including the managed VPN gateways of the major clouds, use IPsec\nnegotiated by IKEv2. Two phases:\n\n1. **IKE SA (authentication and key agreement).** The two gateways exchange Diffie-Hellman values to\n   derive shared keys, then authenticate each other with a pre-shared key or certificates. Certificates\n   are preferable at scale because a leaked PSK compromises every tunnel that shares it. The result is an\n   encrypted control channel, usually over UDP 500, moving to UDP 4500 when NAT is detected (NAT\n   traversal), which is why firewalls on both sides must open those ports. Without NAT, the data\n   packets travel as raw ESP (IP protocol 50, not TCP or UDP), which firewalls must permit as well.\n2. **Child SAs (the data plane).** Inside that channel the peers agree keys and traffic selectors for ESP,\n   the protocol that encrypts and authenticates each packet. Keys are rekeyed on a timer so a single\n   compromised key exposes only a bounded window.\n\n### Routing: policy-based vs route-based\nA policy-based VPN encrypts traffic matching configured source/destination prefixes; adding a subnet\nmeans editing both ends. A route-based VPN presents the tunnel as a virtual interface and uses ordinary\nrouting, either static routes or BGP. BGP is preferred for production because each side advertises its\nprefixes and withdraws them when a tunnel fails, so failover between redundant tunnels happens\nautomatically. Cloud VPN gateways typically give you two tunnels per connection, terminating on\ndifferent endpoints, precisely so that the provider can maintain one without an outage; configure both.\n\n### What the packet pays\nEach packet gains ESP headers, padding and an authentication tag plus an outer IP header, tens of bytes\nin total. A full-size 1,500-byte packet no longer fits, so either it is fragmented or, if the\n\"don't fragment\" bit is set and ICMP is blocked, silently dropped. Gateways therefore clamp the TCP\nmaximum segment size (MSS) in the SYN handshake so endpoints send smaller segments; cloud VPN docs\ntypically recommend a clamp somewhere in the 1,350–1,400-byte range, but use your provider's figure.\nClamping happens at the gateway *because* you cannot rely on every host's path-MTU discovery when\nfirewalls drop the ICMP messages it depends on. It fixes TCP only; UDP traffic must be sized by the\napplication. Throughput per tunnel is also bounded by the\ngateway's crypto capacity and the internet path, typically gigabit-class rather than the tens of gigabits\na dedicated circuit offers (check your provider's documented limits).\n\n### Remote-access VPN and WireGuard\nClient VPNs connect a single device, for example an FDE's laptop, into a customer network. WireGuard is\na newer, deliberately small protocol: fixed modern cryptography, peers identified by public keys, and a\nconfiguration of a few lines. Its simplicity makes it easy to audit and fast, but many enterprises\nstandardize on IPsec appliances, so you meet WireGuard more in your own infrastructure than in a\nbank's.\n\n### When address spaces overlap\nRouting only works if each side can tell local addresses from remote ones, so overlapping CIDRs are\nthe first thing to check, not the last. Three ways out, in rough order of preference: terminate in a\nfresh network whose range neither side uses (cheap when you control one end, as a vendor usually\ndoes); NAT on the gateway, so the customer sees your traffic arriving from an agreed non-overlapping\nrange and you reach their hosts through translated addresses (works, but every log and firewall rule\nnow speaks two address vocabularies); or re-address one side, which is correct and almost never\nquick inside an enterprise.\n\n### Narrowing the blast radius\nBecause a tunnel joins networks, scope it: advertise only the specific subnets needed, put firewall rules\nor security groups on both ends that allow only the required ports to the required hosts, and terminate\nthe tunnel in a dedicated integration VPC rather than your core production network.","example":"A legal-tech vendor pilots contract search for a firm whose 2 TB document archive sits on an on-prem\nfile server, with case metadata in a SQL Server instance. The plan is a site-to-site VPN from the firm's\ndata center to a dedicated \"customer-integration\" VPC in the vendor's cloud account.\n\nAddressing first: the firm uses 10.0.0.0/8 broadly and the vendor's VPC is 10.20.0.0/16, which overlaps.\nRather than re-IP anything, the vendor confirms the firm uses nothing in 172.16.0.0/12, creates the\nintegration VPC as 172.31.240.0/22, and the firm\nadvertises only two /32 host routes (the file server and the database) over BGP. Firewall rules on the\nfirm's side allow only SMB and the SQL Server port, from the ingestion workers' subnet, to those two hosts.\n\nCapacity next. The firm's internet uplink and the tunnel yield an effective 400 Mbit/s for bulk transfer\n(an assumption to measure, not a promise). The initial sync is 2 TB × 8 = 16 Tbit; at 0.4 Gbit/s that is\n40,000 seconds, about 11 hours, so it is scheduled over a weekend with rate limiting to protect the firm's\nbusiness traffic. Daily deltas of around 5 GB take under two minutes.\n\nThen the classic failure: small metadata queries work, but large file reads stall after a few kilobytes.\nPacket captures show oversized packets being dropped with ICMP blocked. Enabling MSS clamping on the\ngateway fixes it. Finally both tunnels are configured, and the vendor tests failover by disabling one\nduring a sync and confirming BGP shifts traffic without dropping the job.","tradeoffs":["A VPN is quick and cheap to stand up over existing internet links, but throughput and latency vary with the internet path; once traffic is large and permanent, a dedicated circuit pays for itself.","Joining networks enables any protocol in either direction, which helps legacy integrations, but it widens the attack surface compared with a one-way private endpoint to a single service.","A dedicated circuit gives predictable bandwidth and latency, but it is private rather than encrypted by default, so regulated customers often still run IPsec (or MACsec where offered) over it; the choice flips back to VPN alone when volumes are modest and lead time matters.","Static routes are simple to reason about for one or two subnets, but they carry no liveness signal of their own, so failover depends on vendor-specific tunnel monitoring (dead-peer detection, route tracking) or manual edits; BGP adds configuration and debugging skill in return for standard, automatic failover and fewer manual edits.","Remote-access VPN gives engineers broad network access easily, but identity-aware zero-trust access per application is safer when many people need narrow access to a few internal tools."],"pitfalls":["Overlapping CIDR ranges discovered after the tunnel is up; the symptom is a healthy tunnel status with traffic that never arrives, because each side routes the other's addresses locally.","No MSS clamping or path MTU discovery when ICMP is filtered; the symptom is small requests succeeding while large uploads, file reads or long model responses hang mid-transfer.","Configuring only one of the two provided tunnels; the symptom is an outage during the provider's routine maintenance window, often in the middle of the night.","Advertising whole corporate supernets \"to keep it simple\"; the symptom shows up in the security review as the vendor network being able to reach thousands of customer hosts it never needed.","Leaving DNS out of the design; the symptom is IP addresses hard-coded in config because internal hostnames do not resolve across the tunnel, which then break when the customer re-IPs a server."],"when":"Use a site-to-site VPN when your cloud workloads need private, bidirectional or multi-protocol access to\na customer's on-prem systems: databases, file shares, legacy APIs, internal identity providers. It is the\ndefault for pilots and moderate steady-state traffic. Prefer a private endpoint when the flow is one-way\nto a single cloud service, and a dedicated circuit when sustained volume or latency guarantees matter.\nFor people rather than systems, prefer per-application zero-trust access over a full-network client VPN.\nMeasure tunnel uptime, BGP state changes, throughput against the plan, and dropped or fragmented\npackets. In interviews and design reviews, mentioning address planning, scope and MTU unprompted signals\nthat you have actually operated one.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures ingestion workers to use internal DNS names over the tunnel, sets timeouts and retries that survive tunnel failover, and recognises an MTU stall from its distinctive symptom.","architect":"Chooses VPN versus private endpoint versus dedicated circuit, plans non-overlapping address space, and decides where the tunnel terminates so its blast radius stays small.","fde":"Fills in the customer's VPN request form with exact peer IPs, prefixes and ports, schedules the bulk sync around their business hours, and joins the call when the tunnel will not come up."},"check":{"q":"Your ingestion job reads from a customer's on-prem database over a new site-to-site VPN. Short\nqueries return instantly, but any query returning more than a few kilobytes hangs until timeout. The\ntunnel shows \"up\". What is happening and how do you confirm and fix it?","a":"That size-dependent pattern points to MTU. IPsec encapsulation adds tens of bytes, so full-size\npackets exceed the tunnel MTU; if \"don't fragment\" is set and ICMP \"fragmentation needed\" messages are\nblocked by a firewall, those packets are silently dropped while small ones pass. Confirm by sending pings\nwith the don't-fragment flag (on Linux, `ping -M do -s 1400 <host>`) at decreasing sizes to find the largest packet that passes, or by capturing\ntraffic and seeing retransmissions of large segments. Fix it by enabling TCP MSS clamping on the VPN\ngateway to a value that fits the tunnel overhead, or by allowing the relevant ICMP messages so path MTU\ndiscovery works. Then retest with a large result set."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do production site-to-site VPNs usually use BGP rather than static routes?","options":["BGP encrypts traffic more strongly than static routing","BGP advertises and withdraws prefixes dynamically, so traffic fails over to the redundant tunnel automatically","Static routes cannot be used with IPsec","BGP removes the need for non-overlapping address ranges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A static route says nothing about whether its tunnel is alive, so failover relies on vendor-specific tunnel monitoring or manual action. BGP withdraws routes from a failed tunnel as part of the protocol. Encryption is ESP's job, and overlapping ranges remain a problem with either routing method."},{"q":"A customer asks for a VPN so your SaaS can call their single internal REST API. Their network team worries about exposure. What is the strongest scoping you can offer?","options":["Use a pre-shared key instead of certificates to keep configuration simple","Advertise the customer's full 10.0.0.0/8 so routing never needs updating","Terminate in a dedicated integration network, advertise only the API host's address, and allow only its port from your worker subnet","Enable split tunneling on every engineer's laptop"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Scoping routes to a single host and filtering ports on both sides limits what the joined networks can reach. Broad advertisements create the exposure the network team fears, and PSK choice affects authentication rather than reach."},{"q":"When is a dedicated circuit such as Direct Connect or ExpressRoute a better choice than a VPN?","options":["When the integration is a two-week pilot that needs to start tomorrow","Whenever the data is confidential, because VPNs are not encrypted","When the customer has overlapping CIDR ranges","When traffic is large and permanent or needs predictable latency and bandwidth that the internet path cannot guarantee"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Dedicated circuits trade long lead times and higher fixed cost for consistent bandwidth and latency. VPNs are encrypted, and a circuit does nothing to fix overlapping address ranges, which still require re-addressing or NAT."}]},{"key":"deployment/terraform","sources":["Official docs: Terraform","Official docs: OpenTofu","Kief Morris, 2020 — Infrastructure as Code (2nd edition)"],"why":"An AI platform is a lot of infrastructure: a VPC with private subnets, private endpoints to the model\nAPI and storage, a Kubernetes cluster with a GPU node pool, a vector database, KMS keys, IAM roles,\nlog sinks. Built by hand in a console, it works once. Then you need a staging copy, then the customer\nwants it in their own account in another region, then an auditor asks who opened port 443 to the world\nand when.\n\nConsole work fails because it leaves no reviewable record, cannot be reproduced exactly, and drifts:\nthe third environment is never quite like the first. Imperative scripts (a shell file of CLI calls) are\nbetter but still brittle, because they encode steps rather than outcomes, so running them twice either\nfails (\"already exists\") or duplicates resources, and they cannot tell you what would change before\nthey change it.\n\nDeclarative IaC fixes this by making the files the source of truth. You state what should exist; the\ntool works out the create, update and delete calls, and can show them to a reviewer first. Changes go\nthrough pull requests like application code, so a security reviewer can approve the diff rather than\ntrust a screenshot. For forward-deployed work this is decisive: the same reviewed module can be run by\nthe customer's own platform team, in their account, under their change control.","how":"### Configuration, providers and the resource graph\nYou write `.tf` files in HCL declaring resources (`aws_vpc_endpoint`, `google_container_node_pool`,\n`azurerm_private_endpoint`) with arguments. Providers are plugins that translate each resource type into\nAPI calls for a cloud or service; that plugin model is why one tool covers AWS, Azure, Google Cloud,\nKubernetes, DNS providers and many SaaS products. Terraform parses references between resources\n(a security group ID used by an endpoint) into a dependency graph, then creates independent resources in\nparallel and dependent ones in order. That is why you rarely write ordering by hand.\n\n### State\nTerraform records what it created, and the real IDs, in a state file. State exists because the tool\nmust map `aws_s3_bucket.documents` in your code to one specific bucket in the account, and must know\nwhich resources it owns so it can delete one you removed from the code. Teams store state remotely\n(object storage, Terraform Cloud/HCP, or similar) with locking so two applies cannot run at once and\ncorrupt it. State contains every attribute the provider returns, including generated passwords and\nkeys, in plain text, so it must be encrypted and access-controlled like a secret.\n\n### Plan and apply\n`terraform plan` refreshes state from the real APIs, compares with your configuration and prints the\ncreate/update/replace/destroy actions. \"Replace\" is the line to read carefully, because some argument\nchanges cannot be updated in place and force destroy-then-create, which for a database or bucket means\ndata loss. `terraform apply` executes a plan; in CI you save the plan file and apply exactly that plan\nafter approval, so what was reviewed is what runs.\n\n### Modules and variables\nModules package resources behind input variables and outputs, like functions. A \"customer deployment\"\nmodule might take a VPC ID, subnet IDs, region and KMS key, and output the service URL. Each environment\nor customer is then a small root configuration calling that module with different inputs.\n\n### How the diff decides update versus replace\nFor each resource the plan compares three things: the configuration, the last-recorded state, and what\nthe refresh read from the API. If an argument differs, the provider's schema says whether the API can\nchange it in place (a tag, an instance count) or whether it is immutable once created (a bucket name,\na cluster's subnet), in which case the action becomes replace. Replacement defaults to\ndestroy-then-create *because* many names must be unique; `lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }`\nreverses the order for stateless things like launch templates or certificates, so there is no gap.\n\n### Resource addresses are identity\nState keys each object by its address in code (`module.rag.aws_s3_bucket.documents`), not by its\ncloud ID. Renaming a block, moving it into a module or switching `count` to `for_each` therefore looks\nto Terraform like \"delete the old address, create a new one\", even though nothing real changed. A\n`moved { from = ..., to = ... }` block (or `terraform state mv`) tells it the object merely changed\naddress, and the plan becomes a no-op. This is the mechanism behind most \"harmless refactor recreated\nthe database\" stories.\n\n### Drift and import\nIf someone changes a resource in the console, the next plan shows drift and proposes reverting it.\nExisting hand-built resources can be adopted with an `import` block or `terraform import` so they come\nunder management without being recreated.\n\n### One state per environment\nEach environment or customer gets its own state, so a plan for staging cannot touch production.\nTwo layouts are common: separate root directories per environment (explicit, each with its own\nbackend key and credentials) or CLI workspaces sharing one configuration (less duplication, but the\nsame credentials and an easy-to-forget `terraform workspace select`). For customer accounts with\nseparate change boards, separate roots with separate backends is the usual choice *because* the\nisolation boundary should match the approval boundary.\n\n### Alternatives\nCloudFormation (AWS) and Bicep/ARM (Azure) are native single-cloud equivalents with no state file to\nmanage, since the provider tracks it. Pulumi and AWS CDK let you write infrastructure in general-purpose\nlanguages, which helps loops and abstraction but makes reviews depend on reading code rather than a\ndeclarative file. OpenTofu is a community fork of Terraform created after HashiCorp moved Terraform to\nthe Business Source License in 2023; it keeps HCL and the provider ecosystem and is open source.","example":"A minimal module for the private model-access slice of a customer deployment: a locked remote state, a\nsecurity group that only admits the app subnet, an interface endpoint for the model runtime with private\nDNS, and a document bucket protected from accidental destruction. Argument names follow the AWS provider;\ncheck them against the provider version you pin.\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  required_providers {\n    aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.0\" }\n  }\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"acme-tfstate\"\n    key            = \"ai-platform/prod.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"eu-west-1\"\n    use_lockfile   = true         # S3-native lock file (added in 1.10, GA in 1.11); supersedes dynamodb_table\n    encrypt        = true\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"vpc_id\"             { type = string }\nvariable \"private_subnet_ids\" { type = list(string) }\nvariable \"app_cidr\"           { type = string }\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"model_ep\" {\n  name   = \"model-runtime-endpoint\"\n  vpc_id = var.vpc_id\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = \"tcp\"\n    cidr_blocks = [var.app_cidr]\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_vpc_endpoint\" \"model_runtime\" {\n  vpc_id              = var.vpc_id\n  service_name        = \"com.amazonaws.eu-west-1.bedrock-runtime\"\n  vpc_endpoint_type   = \"Interface\"\n  subnet_ids          = var.private_subnet_ids     # one subnet per availability zone\n  security_group_ids  = [aws_security_group.model_ep.id]\n  private_dns_enabled = true\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"documents\" {\n  bucket = \"acme-rag-documents-prod\"\n  lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }   # plan fails instead of deleting customer data\n}\n```\n\n`terraform plan` on a fresh account shows three resources to add; changing `bucket` later would show a\nreplace, and `prevent_destroy` turns that into an error rather than a deleted archive. The guard lives in\nthe resource block, so it does not protect against someone deleting the block itself; pair it with\nbucket versioning or a deletion-protection setting on the cloud side.","tradeoffs":["Declarative plans make every change reviewable before it happens, but the state file becomes critical shared infrastructure; losing or corrupting it is worse than losing the code.","One tool across clouds and SaaS providers helps teams deploying into varied customer accounts, but a single-cloud team may find native CloudFormation or Bicep simpler since there is no state to operate.","HCL's restricted language keeps configurations readable for security reviewers, but complex logic (dynamic topologies, heavy loops) is awkward; general-purpose IaC tools win when logic dominates.","Large single state files give one view of everything but make plans slow and blast radius big; splitting by layer (network, cluster, app) limits damage at the cost of cross-state references."],"pitfalls":["Committing state or secrets to git, or storing state unencrypted; the symptom is a secret scanner alert, or an auditor finding database passwords readable by everyone with repository access.","Approving a plan without reading \"must be replaced\" lines; the symptom is a vector database or bucket recreated empty after an apparently harmless argument change.","Refactoring resource names or module structure without `moved` blocks; the symptom is a plan that shows an equal number of destroys and creates for a pull request that \"only renamed things\".","Unpinned provider versions or an uncommitted `.terraform.lock.hcl`; the symptom is a plan that suddenly proposes changes nobody made, after a new provider release changed a default.","Making emergency fixes in the console and never back-porting them; the symptom is the next routine apply silently reverting the fix and re-causing the incident.","Assuming the customer's air-gapped environment can download providers; the symptom is `terraform init` failing, fixed only by shipping a provider mirror with the release bundle."],"when":"Use IaC for anything that must exist in more than one environment or survive an audit: which is to say\nevery production AI deployment, and every customer-VPC deployment. Terraform or OpenTofu is the default\nwhen you deploy across clouds or into many customer accounts, or when the customer's platform team\nalready uses it. Use native templates when an organization is single-cloud and mandates them. Keep\napplication rollout (container versions, model weights) in a deploy tool such as Helm or Argo rather than\nTerraform, since those change far more often. Measure drift (plans that are not empty on main) and\ntime to stand up a new environment. Interviewers probe state, plan review and the replace hazard.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes modules for GPU node pools, endpoints and buckets, runs plan in CI on every pull request, and reads each replace or destroy action before approving.","architect":"Sets the state layout, module boundaries and versioning, decides which layer IaC owns versus the deploy tool, and makes plan output part of the change-approval evidence.","fde":"Hands the customer a versioned module and variables file their platform team can run in their own account, plus a provider mirror when their environment cannot reach the registry."},"check":{"q":"A customer's platform team must run your deployment in their own AWS account under their change\nboard, and they forbid your engineers from having write access. How do you structure the Terraform\ndelivery so it is reviewable, repeatable and safe for them to run?","a":"Ship a versioned module with a narrow, documented variable interface (VPC and subnet IDs, KMS key,\nregion, sizing), no hard-coded account details, and pinned provider versions. Their root configuration\ncalls it, with state in their backend under their access controls and locking. Their CI runs plan and\nattaches the saved plan to the change ticket; the board reviews it, and apply executes exactly that\nplan. Protect data resources with `prevent_destroy`, document every replace-triggering argument, and\npublish an IAM policy listing the permissions the apply role needs. For upgrades, release a new module\nversion with a changelog and an expected-plan summary so they can compare. If the account is isolated,\ninclude a provider mirror."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Terraform need a state file at all, when it could just query the cloud APIs?","options":["To cache API responses so plans run faster","To map each resource in the code to the specific real object it manages, including ones removed from code that must be deleted","Because cloud APIs do not report resource attributes","To store the HCL source in a binary form"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without state, Terraform cannot know which real bucket corresponds to a resource block, or which objects it owns when a block is deleted. Refreshing does query the APIs, but only for objects state identifies."},{"q":"A reviewer sees a plan line saying the vector database cluster \"must be replaced\" after a minor-looking argument change. What is the right response?","options":["Approve it, since Terraform preserves data during replacement","Run apply twice so the second run cancels the replacement","Treat it as destroy-and-recreate with data loss, and find an in-place path or a planned migration before applying","Delete the state file so Terraform forgets the cluster exists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replacement destroys the existing resource and creates a new one, so stored data is lost unless migrated. Deleting state would make Terraform try to create a duplicate and abandon the original unmanaged."},{"q":"Why might an AWS-only team reasonably prefer CloudFormation over Terraform?","options":["CloudFormation supports more third-party SaaS providers","Terraform cannot manage AWS networking resources","CloudFormation lets you write infrastructure in Python","AWS tracks the stack state itself, so there is no separate state file and locking setup to operate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The native service manages state and rollback for you, which removes an operational burden. Terraform's advantage is breadth across clouds and SaaS, and Python-based infrastructure belongs to CDK or Pulumi."}]},{"key":"deployment/security-questionnaires","sources":["Official docs: Cloud Security Alliance — Consensus Assessments Initiative Questionnaire (CAIQ)","Official docs: Shared Assessments — Standardized Information Gathering (SIG) questionnaire","Official docs: AICPA — SOC 2"],"why":"A large company may have thousands of vendors, each a possible path to a breach. Its risk team cannot\nsend engineers to inspect each one, so it standardizes the inquiry: a questionnaire, either an industry\ntemplate such as the Shared Assessments SIG or the Cloud Security Alliance's CAIQ, or a custom spreadsheet\nbuilt from the buyer's own control framework. Answers are scored, gaps become findings, and serious\ngaps block procurement or become contract clauses.\n\nFor AI vendors the questionnaire carries extra weight because the product's core function is sending\ncustomer data to a model. Buyers ask questions they did not ask a CRM vendor: is our data used to train\nany model, which model providers see it, how long do they retain prompts, can your agent take actions,\nhow do you stop prompt injection from exfiltrating data.\n\nThe naive approaches fail. Having whoever gets the spreadsheet answer it from memory produces\ninconsistent answers across customers, which a sophisticated buyer or a later dispute will expose.\nAnswering \"Yes\" to everything to unblock the deal creates misrepresentation risk, because questionnaires\nare often incorporated by reference into the contract. And treating it as paperwork separate from\nengineering means answers drift from the system as it changes. The questionnaire is really a structured\ninterface between your actual controls and the buyer's risk model, and it should be maintained like one.","how":"### What arrives\nTypically a spreadsheet or a portal from a third-party risk platform, with yes/no/not-applicable columns,\na free-text comment and an evidence-upload field per question. Questions cluster into domains:\ngovernance and policies, access control and identity, encryption in transit and at rest, key\nmanagement, network security, vulnerability management and penetration testing, logging and\nmonitoring, incident response and notification, business continuity, data retention and deletion,\nprivacy, subprocessors, secure development, and increasingly an AI section.\n\n### What happens on the buyer's side\nUnderstanding the reviewer's workflow explains what they reward:\n1. **Inherent-risk tiering.** Before sending anything, the risk team classifies the engagement by what\n   you will touch: data sensitivity, volume, whether you get network or system access, and how\n   critical you are to operations. A vendor that processes patient records with agent tool access\n   lands in the top tier; a vendor that sees only marketing copy does not. The tier sets the depth,\n   *because* the team's review hours are the scarce resource. (Templates are built for this: the SIG\n   comes in a shorter Lite and a fuller Core form, and CAIQ maps each question to a control in the CSA\n   Cloud Controls Matrix.)\n2. **Scoring and findings.** Each answer is compared with the buyer's required control. A \"No\" or a\n   vague answer on a required control becomes a finding with a severity.\n3. **Follow-up and evidence requests.** Reviewers ask for proof on the answers that matter most to\n   their tier; precise first answers mean fewer rounds.\n4. **Disposition.** Findings are accepted as risk, mitigated by a compensating control, or turned into\n   remediation commitments with dates, which often land in the contract.\n5. **Reassessment.** High-tier vendors are typically re-reviewed periodically and on material change,\n   so an answer you give today will be compared with the one you give next year.\n\nThe practical upshot: a reviewer is looking for residual risk they can sign off on, not a perfect\nscore, so a clearly scoped gap with a mitigation is easier to approve than an ambiguous \"Yes\".\n\n### Evidence that shortcuts it\nBuyers accept independent assurance in place of many answers. A SOC 2 Type II report is an auditor's\nopinion on whether your controls operated effectively over a period (Type I covers design at a point in\ntime only). An ISO/IEC 27001 certificate shows a certified information security management system. A\nrecent penetration test summary, a public subprocessor list and a trust center page answer many questions\nat once. Sending the report early, under NDA, often removes a large share of the questionnaire.\n\n### The answer library\nMature vendors keep a single library of approved answers, each tied to the underlying control, an owner\n(security, infrastructure, legal, ML), the evidence that proves it and a review date. New questionnaires\nare answered by matching each question to library entries and adapting wording. This is a retrieval\nproblem, and many teams use RAG over the library to draft responses, but a human owner must approve\neach answer, because a confidently wrong generated answer is precisely the misrepresentation you are\ntrying to avoid.\n\n### Writing a good answer\nState the fact, its scope, and the mechanism, and say where it is evidenced. Answer the question asked,\nnot a neighbouring one. When the honest answer is \"No\" or \"Partially\", say so and add the compensating\ncontrol or a dated roadmap item; reviewers deal with gaps constantly, but discovered misstatements\ndestroy trust. Use \"Not applicable\" only with a one-line reason.\n\n### The AI section\nExpect questions on: training use of customer data (yours and every model provider's), prompt and\noutput retention by each subprocessor, region of processing, model provenance and evaluation, human\noversight, guardrails against prompt injection and data leakage, tool permissions for agents, and how\ncustomers can delete their data including embeddings and caches. Answer these per data flow, because the\nhonest answer often differs between the hosted-API path and a self-hosted model path.","example":"A health-tech buyer sends 300 questions. Three show the difference between a weak and a strong answer.\n\n\"Is customer data used to train AI models?\" Weak: \"No.\" Strong: \"No. Customer prompts, documents and\noutputs are not used to train or fine-tune our models. Our LLM subprocessor processes data under API\nterms that exclude training use; its retention terms and region are listed on our subprocessor page.\nEvidence: DPA section 4, subprocessor list.\" The strong version covers the subprocessor, which is what\nthe buyer really wants to know.\n\n\"Is all data encrypted at rest with customer-managed keys?\" The truth: data at rest is encrypted with\nprovider-managed keys; customer-managed keys exist only on the dedicated-deployment tier. Answer\n\"Partially\", explain both tiers, and point to the dedicated option. Saying \"Yes\" would be true for one\ntier and false for the one this customer bought.\n\n\"Do you retain prompts and responses?\" The engineers check and find traces with full prompts kept for\n30 days in the observability tool, which nobody had listed. The answer becomes: \"Yes, for 30 days for\ndebugging, access-restricted, deletable on request,\" and the observability vendor is added to the\nsubprocessor list. The questionnaire surfaced a real gap before an audit did.\n\nOverall, the SOC 2 report removed about a third of the questions as covered (an illustrative proportion),\nand the remaining answers were drafted from the library and approved by owners within the week.","tradeoffs":["Industry templates such as SIG or CAIQ let you answer once and reuse widely, but buyers with their own frameworks will still send custom forms, so the library must map to both.","Independent reports like SOC 2 shortcut many questions and carry weight, but they cost significant time and money annually; for an early-stage vendor with few enterprise deals, a strong library may suffice first.","Detailed, specific answers build trust and reduce follow-ups, but each specific detail is a commitment; where the system changes often, describe the control and its guarantee rather than its exact implementation.","AI-drafted answers from the library save hours per questionnaire, but they only work when every generated answer is reviewed by the owner of the underlying control."],"pitfalls":["Answering aspirationally to unblock a deal; the symptom is a later audit or incident where the contract-referenced answer is shown to be false, turning a technical gap into a legal one.","Forgetting subprocessors in the data-flow story, especially model providers and observability tools; the symptom is a buyer finding an unlisted vendor in your trace exports or DNS logs.","Letting answers diverge across customers; the symptom is two customers comparing notes, or the same question answered differently in a renewal, prompting a full re-review.","Never revisiting answers after architecture changes; the symptom is stale claims, for example \"data never leaves the EU\" after someone added a US-hosted model fallback route.","Attaching sensitive evidence without controls, such as a full penetration-test report with unfixed findings or a detailed network diagram, to a portal outside an NDA; the symptom is your internal security detail circulating among people you have never met. Share summaries and attestation letters by default, full reports under NDA on request.","Treating \"not applicable\" as a shortcut; the symptom is reviewer follow-ups asking why, which slow the review more than a one-line justified answer would have."],"when":"Every sale to a regulated or large enterprise passes through one, so prepare before the first deal: write\nthe answer library, the subprocessor list, a data-flow diagram and the AI data-use statement while the\narchitecture is fresh. Pursue SOC 2 or ISO 27001 when the volume of questionnaires and deal size justify\nthe audit cost. Route every questionnaire through the owner of each control rather than the account\nteam alone. Measure turnaround time, follow-up rounds and the number of answers changed after\nengineering review. In interviews for FDE and architect roles, expect \"how do you answer a question\nwhere the honest answer is no?\" The answer is: honestly, with compensating controls and a dated plan.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns accurate answers about the systems they build, such as retention in traces, embedding deletion and model routing, and flags when a change invalidates a published answer.","architect":"Designs data flows so the answers are good ones, for example a no-retention model path or a region-pinned tier, and keeps the authoritative data-flow diagram the answers rest on.","fde":"Shepherds the customer's questionnaire through internal owners, explains gaps and compensating controls on calls, and feeds recurring asks back to product as roadmap items."},"check":{"q":"A prospect's questionnaire asks \"Does any customer data leave the EU?\" Your app runs in an EU\nregion, but its fallback model route goes to a US-hosted provider when the EU model is rate-limited, and\nyour error tracker is US-hosted. How do you answer, and what do you do beyond answering?","a":"The honest answer is \"Yes, in specific cases,\" followed by each flow: the fallback route may send\nprompts to a US region when the EU model is throttled, and error events may contain request fragments sent\nto a US-hosted tracker. Name the subprocessors, the transfer mechanism in the DPA, and any mitigations such\nas scrubbing payloads from error events. Beyond the answer, offer a configuration that keeps data in the EU:\ndisable cross-region fallback for this tenant, accepting more throttling or degraded responses, and switch\nthe tracker to an EU instance or strip content. Then update the answer library and subprocessor list so the\nnext questionnaire is correct, and tell engineering the routing change has compliance consequences."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does sending a SOC 2 Type II report early often shorten a security questionnaire?","options":["It certifies the product is free of vulnerabilities","It is an independent auditor's opinion that controls operated over a period, which buyers accept in place of many individual answers","It replaces the need for a data processing agreement","It proves compliance with HIPAA and GDPR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Type II report gives third-party assurance on control operation over time, so buyers can mark whole domains as covered. It is not a vulnerability guarantee, a legal agreement or proof of any regulation."},{"q":"The honest answer to \"Do you support customer-managed encryption keys?\" is \"only on our dedicated tier,\" and this buyer is on the shared tier. What is the best response?","options":["Answer \"Yes\", since the product supports the feature","Answer \"Not applicable\", since this buyer did not purchase that tier","Answer \"Partially\", explain which tier offers it and what protects data on the shared tier","Leave the question blank and raise it on a call later"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A scoped, accurate answer with the alternative is both honest and useful. \"Yes\" would misstate this buyer's protection, and \"not applicable\" is wrong because the question applies to them."},{"q":"Your team uses an LLM over the answer library to draft questionnaire responses. What control matters most?","options":["Using the largest available model to reduce errors","Letting the model answer only yes/no questions","Re-running the model until two drafts agree","Requiring the owner of each underlying control to approve the drafted answer before it is sent"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Retrieval speeds drafting, but answers are commitments, and a fluent wrong answer is a misrepresentation. Model size and agreement between drafts do not establish truth; an accountable owner's review does."}]},{"key":"deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","sources":["Adam Shostack, 2014 — Threat Modeling: Designing for Security","Official docs: OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","Official docs: NIST AI Risk Management Framework"],"why":"A questionnaire tells the buyer what you say your controls are. It cannot tell them how your system\nfits into theirs: which of their networks it touches, what identity it uses to read their SharePoint,\nwhether an agent could email a customer, where an LLM prompt containing patient data is logged. Those\nquestions depend on the specific deployment, so large organizations route new systems through an\narchitecture review board and a security review before production access is granted. Internally, good\nengineering teams run the same kind of review on their own designs before building.\n\nWithout a review, problems surface in the worst places: an over-privileged service account noticed during\nan incident, a data path to an unapproved region found by an auditor, an agent's tool that can modify\nrecords discovered by a user. Fixing design problems after deployment costs far more than changing a\ndiagram.\n\nThe naive way to \"pass\" is to show up with marketing slides and answer questions live. That fails\nbecause reviewers are looking for specifics: every data store, every boundary crossing, every credential.\nVague answers produce a long list of open actions and a second meeting weeks later. AI systems make this\nharder, because reviewers are often meeting LLM-specific risks for the first time and need you to explain\nhow the model can be manipulated and what bounds the damage. The vendor who brings that analysis\nunprompted earns trust that a slide deck cannot.","how":"### The typical flow\n1. **Intake.** The sponsor files a request with a system description, data classification and owners.\n   Data classification sets the depth of review: a system touching regulated personal data gets more\n   scrutiny than an internal FAQ bot.\n2. **Pre-read package.** You submit documents in advance, because reviewers want to come to the meeting\n   with questions, not discover the design in it.\n3. **Review session.** Reviewers walk the diagram, probe boundaries and ask \"what happens if\" questions.\n4. **Findings.** Each finding gets a severity, an owner and an outcome: fix before go-live, fix by a\n   date, accept the risk with a named approver, or mitigate with a compensating control.\n5. **Decision.** Approve, approve with conditions, or reject; conditions are tracked to closure,\n   usually in the customer's risk register.\n\n### What the pre-read contains\n- **Architecture diagram** at container level (C4-style is common): every service, data store, external\n  dependency and trust boundary, including model providers and observability tools.\n- **Data flow diagram** for each sensitive flow: what data, from where, to where, encrypted how, retained\n  how long. This is the document reviewers scrutinize most, because breaches follow data.\n- **Identity and access:** how users authenticate (their SSO), how services authenticate to each other and to\n  customer systems, which permissions each role and service account has, and why.\n- **Network:** ingress and egress paths, private endpoints, VPNs, what is publicly reachable.\n- **Encryption and keys:** in transit, at rest, who holds keys, whether customer-managed keys are used.\n- **Logging and monitoring:** what is logged, where, for how long, whether prompts or outputs are included.\n- **Threat model:** for example STRIDE per boundary, plus AI-specific threats from the OWASP LLM list:\n  prompt injection (direct and via retrieved documents), sensitive information disclosure, excessive\n  agency of tools, improper output handling (called insecure output handling in the 2023 list), vector\n  and embedding weaknesses, and supply chain of models and datasets.\n- **Evidence:** penetration test summary, vulnerability scanning, SBOM, SOC 2 or ISO reports.\n\n### Applying STRIDE at a boundary\nA threat model is not a list of scary words; it is the same six questions asked at every place data or\ncontrol crosses a trust boundary. STRIDE names them: **S**poofing (can someone pretend to be a\nlegitimate caller?), **T**ampering (can data be altered in transit or at rest?), **R**epudiation (could\nan actor deny an action because nothing recorded it?), **I**nformation disclosure (can data reach\nsomeone not entitled to it?), **D**enial of service (can the path be exhausted?) and **E**levation of\nprivilege (can a caller gain rights it was not given?). Take one boundary in a RAG agent, \"retrieved\ndocument text enters the model prompt\": tampering is a planted instruction in a document (indirect\nprompt injection); information disclosure is the model quoting a passage the user may not see;\nelevation of privilege is the injected instruction causing a tool call with the agent's broader\ncredentials; repudiation is an action with no log tying it to a user and a source document. Each\nanswer points to a control, which is exactly what the reviewer wants to see written down. The method\nworks *because* boundaries are finite and enumerable, while \"think of attacks\" is not.\n\n### The architecture half\nAn architecture review board asks questions a security reviewer may not: does the design use approved\ntechnologies and integration patterns, or does it introduce a new database the customer's operations\nteam has never run? What are the availability target and the recovery point and time objectives, and\ndoes the design meet them? Who operates it, and does its monitoring land in their tooling? What does it\ncost at expected volume, and how does that scale? How would they leave: can data and indexes be\nexported if the vendor is replaced? These questions exist *because* the customer will live with the\nsystem long after the project team moves on, so fit with their estate matters as much as its\ninternal elegance.\n\n### AI-specific questions to prepare for\nReviewers increasingly ask: can a document in the index instruct the model to leak other documents? Does\nretrieval respect the user's permissions in the source system? Which tools can the agent call, with\nwhose credentials, and which actions need human approval? What happens when the model is wrong, and how\nwould you know? Can a user's data be deleted from indexes, caches and fine-tuned weights? Prepared\nanswers trace each risk to a control: permission-filtered retrieval, least-privilege tool credentials,\napproval gates, output validation, evaluation results and monitoring.\n\n### Your own reviews\nRun the same process internally before the customer's: a design review with a peer architect and a\nsecurity engineer catches the obvious findings cheaply, and decisions are recorded as ADRs so the\nreasoning survives staff changes.","example":"An FDE prepares a bank's architecture review for an internal-policy assistant that retrieves from\nSharePoint and can draft, but not send, emails. The pre-read has a container diagram, two data-flow\ndiagrams (indexing and query), an access matrix and a threat model. The review produces five findings:\n\n1. **High:** the indexing service account can read all SharePoint sites, so retrieval would ignore\n   per-user permissions. Fix before go-live: each chunk carries the source document's access list captured at\n   index time, retrieval filters by the user's group memberships synced from the identity provider, and the indexer's scope is reduced to the approved site collections. The residual risk,\n   noted in the finding, is staleness: a permission revoked in SharePoint still applies in the index\n   until the next sync, so the sync interval becomes a documented, agreed number.\n2. **High:** full prompts and retrieved passages are written to the vendor-hosted trace store. Fix before\n   go-live: traces stay in the bank's log platform; vendor telemetry receives only metadata.\n3. **Medium:** indirect prompt injection through a malicious document could make the assistant produce\n   a misleading draft. Mitigation accepted: drafts are never auto-sent, the UI marks generated text, and\n   red-team results on injection are attached as evidence.\n4. **Medium:** no documented response if the model provider changes the model version. Fix by date:\n   pin the model version and add a regression eval gate before upgrades.\n5. **Low:** the internal ingress still accepts TLS 1.2 with legacy CBC-mode cipher suites, while the\n   bank's standard requires AEAD-only suites. Risk accepted by the bank's security lead with a review in\n   six months.\n\nTwo highs are closed within two weeks, with evidence (a permission-filter test showing user A cannot\nretrieve user B's restricted file, and a log-flow screenshot). The board approves with conditions on\nfindings 4 and 5, tracked in the bank's risk register.","tradeoffs":["Engaging reviewers early, at design stage, finds structural problems while they are cheap, but the design may change and need re-review; late engagement saves meetings but risks findings that force a redesign.","A thorough pre-read shortens the meeting and the follow-up cycle, but it takes days to prepare; for a low-risk internal tool, a lightweight review with a short template is proportionate.","Accepting risks with a named approver keeps launches moving, but accumulated accepted risks become invisible debt unless they carry expiry dates and are re-reviewed.","Standard frameworks such as STRIDE and the OWASP LLM list give reviewers shared vocabulary, but checklists miss deployment-specific risks; the data-flow walkthrough is what finds those."],"pitfalls":["Bringing a diagram that does not match production; the symptom is a reviewer asking about a service that the diagram omits, and the review losing confidence in everything else presented.","Omitting the model provider and observability vendors from data flows; the symptom is a high-severity finding for undisclosed data transfer, often discovered from a network-egress question.","Agreeing to findings without owners and dates; the symptom is the same findings reappearing at the next review, or blocking go-live at the last minute.","Treating the review as a one-time gate; the symptom is a later feature, such as a new agent tool or a cross-region fallback, shipping without review and being flagged by an audit.","Arguing every finding; the symptom is a reviewer escalating to a reject, when proposing a compensating control or a dated fix would have closed it."],"when":"Expect a customer review for any system touching confidential or regulated data, integrating with\nidentity or internal systems, or taking actions. Run your own internal review for every new system and\nfor significant changes: new data sources, new tools for an agent, new model providers, new regions.\nScale the depth to data classification and blast radius. Measure time from intake to approval, the\nnumber of high findings per review (it should fall as your template matures) and the percentage of\nfindings closed by their due date. In interviews, a strong candidate describes the pre-read package and\nnames AI-specific threats with concrete controls, rather than claiming the system is secure.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the evidence reviewers ask for, such as a test proving permission-filtered retrieval and log configuration excerpts, and implements fixes against named findings.","architect":"Owns the diagrams, data flows, threat model and ADRs, presents the design, and negotiates findings into fixes, mitigations or formally accepted risks with expiry dates.","fde":"Navigates the customer's review process, books the right reviewers, translates their concerns into engineering tickets and keeps the findings tracker moving until approval."},"check":{"q":"In a customer's review, the security architect asks: \"If someone uploads a document containing\ninstructions to the model, what stops your agent from emailing our customer list to an outside address?\"\nWhat is a strong answer?","a":"Acknowledge that indirect prompt injection cannot be fully prevented at the model level, then show\nlayered controls that bound impact. The agent's email tool can only create drafts, so a human must send;\nrecipients are restricted to internal domains by the tool, not the prompt; the agent's credentials cannot\nread the customer list because retrieval is filtered by the requesting user's permissions and the CRM is not\nconnected; outputs are validated and links stripped; and actions are logged for audit. Add evidence: red-team\nresults for injection attempts and the tool's permission policy. Then offer monitoring and an alert on\nanomalous tool use. The key point is that safety rests on capability limits, not on the model obeying."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which pre-read document do reviewers usually scrutinize most closely, and why?","options":["The product roadmap, because it shows future security features","The data-flow diagrams, because they show what sensitive data crosses which boundaries and where it is stored","The penetration test report, because it proves there are no vulnerabilities","The pricing sheet, because it determines the review tier"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Breaches and compliance problems follow data, so reviewers trace each sensitive flow across trust boundaries. A penetration test is useful evidence but shows only what testers found, not the absence of flaws."},{"q":"A review finding says the indexing service account can read every SharePoint site. Why is this rated high for a RAG assistant?","options":["Because SharePoint APIs are rate-limited","Because broad read access slows down indexing","Because the account password might expire","Because without per-user permission filtering at retrieval, any user could receive content they are not entitled to read"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The assistant would answer from documents the requesting user cannot access, bypassing the source system's permissions. The fix is permission-aware retrieval and a narrower indexing scope, not tuning throughput."},{"q":"A finding cannot be fixed before launch. What is the most appropriate way to proceed?","options":["Remove it from the findings list since it cannot be fixed now","Launch and revisit it if an incident occurs","Record a risk acceptance or compensating control with a named approver and a review or fix date","Ask the reviewer to downgrade the severity so it does not block"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Formal acceptance makes the decision visible and owned, and a date ensures it is revisited. Deleting or downgrading the finding hides the risk, which is what the review exists to prevent."}]},{"key":"deployment/compliance-constraints","sources":["Official docs: EU General Data Protection Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2016/679)","Official docs: US HHS — HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules","Official docs: EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689)","Official docs: AICPA — SOC 2"],"why":"A model-backed feature can be technically finished and still be undeployable. A hospital cannot send\npatient notes to a model provider that has not signed a business associate agreement. A European insurer\nmay need contractual safeguards before prompts containing customer data leave the EU. A defense\ncontractor's data may be export-controlled, so only certain personnel may touch the system. None of these\nare engineering preferences; they are obligations whose breach means fines, lost certifications, voided\ncontracts or worse.\n\nThe naive approach is to build first and \"do compliance\" at the end, with a policy document and a\nchecklist. That fails because compliance requirements are architectural. Data residency determines which\nregions and model endpoints you may use. A no-training, limited-retention requirement determines which\nmodel providers qualify. An erasure requirement determines whether you may fine-tune on customer data,\nsince deleting a record from model weights is not practical. An audit requirement determines what your\nlogs must contain, while a minimization requirement limits what they may contain. Discovering these after\nthe build means rework, sometimes a different deployment model entirely, such as moving from SaaS into the\ncustomer's VPC.\n\nThe useful skill is translation: hearing \"we are subject to HIPAA and SOC 2\" and turning it into a short\nlist of concrete constraints the design must satisfy and the evidence it must produce.","how":"### From obligation to evidence\nEvery compliance requirement flows through the same chain: **obligation** (a law, contract clause or\nframework criterion) → **constraint** on the system → **control** that enforces it → **evidence** that the\ncontrol works. Auditors and reviewers check the last link, so design controls that produce evidence\nnaturally: configuration in code, logs, test results.\n\n### The regimes you will meet most\n- **GDPR (EU personal data).** Requires a lawful basis, purpose limitation and data minimization, data\n  processing agreements with processors, honoring rights such as erasure and access, and safeguards\n  for transfers outside the EEA (adequacy decisions or mechanisms such as standard contractual clauses).\n  It does not strictly mandate EU hosting, but many customers impose EU residency contractually because\n  it is the simplest way to stay safe. Two points change designs: the customer is usually the\n  **controller** and you the **processor**, so you act only on their documented instructions and\n  your subprocessors (model, cloud, tracing) need their approval; and European regulators generally\n  treat *remote access* from outside the EEA, such as a US support engineer opening an EU tenant's\n  traces, as a transfer, so residency is about who can read the data, not only where disks sit.\n- **HIPAA (US health data).** Covered entities may share protected health information with a vendor only\n  under a business associate agreement, and that obligation flows down to the vendor's subprocessors,\n  including model and cloud providers. Security Rule safeguards cover access control, audit controls and\n  transmission security.\n- **SOC 2 and ISO/IEC 27001.** Not laws but assurance frameworks customers require of vendors. They turn\n  into constraints on change management, access reviews, logging, incident response and vendor management.\n- **PCI DSS (payment cards).** Any system that stores, processes or transmits cardholder data is in scope;\n  the usual design move is keeping card data out of prompts and logs so the AI system stays out of scope.\n- **Public sector and defense.** US federal cloud use requires FedRAMP-authorized services at an\n  appropriate impact level; export-controlled data (for example under ITAR) restricts who may access it,\n  which often pushes deployments on-prem or air-gapped.\n- **EU AI Act.** A risk-based regulation that phases in obligations over several years: certain practices\n  are prohibited, high-risk systems carry requirements for risk management, data governance, logging,\n  human oversight and transparency, and general-purpose model providers have their own duties. Which tier\n  a use case falls into is a legal determination, so involve counsel.\n- **Sector rules.** Financial services add model risk management expectations (validation, monitoring,\n  documentation); many industries add record-keeping rules that set minimum retention.\n\n### The constraint categories\nTranslate any regime into these questions: **where** may data be processed and stored; **who** may access\nit, including vendors and personnel; **what** data may enter prompts, logs and indexes; **how long** must\nor may it be kept; **how** must it be protected (encryption, customer-managed keys); **what proof** must\nexist (audit trails, change records); and **AI-specific** rules (no training use, human oversight,\ndisclosure that users are talking to AI).\n\n### Enforce at a chokepoint, check in the pipeline\nA constraint written only in a policy document is broken by the first engineer who adds a fallback\nregion. So each constraint should be enforced where every request must pass, and checked where every\nchange must pass:\n- **Runtime chokepoint.** Route all model calls through one gateway that holds a per-tenant policy\n  (allowed providers, allowed regions, whether content may be logged) and refuses anything else.\n  It is a chokepoint *because* a single enforcement point cannot be bypassed by a new code path the\n  way scattered checks can, and its configuration plus its refusal logs are the evidence.\n- **Change-time check.** Run policy checks on infrastructure plans (for example, fail any Terraform\n  plan that creates storage outside approved regions or a log sink without a retention setting), and\n  require review when a new subprocessor appears in the dependency or egress list. This catches the\n  violation before it reaches production, which is when it is cheapest.\n- **Periodic proof.** Access reviews, deletion tests that confirm an erased record is gone from the\n  database, the vector index and the caches, and a reconciliation of actual egress against the\n  declared subprocessor list.\n\n### Constraints conflict\nAudit retention can collide with erasure; debug logging with minimization; a cross-region fallback with\nresidency; fine-tuning on customer data with deletion. Resolve conflicts explicitly in the design, for\nexample by logging metadata and hashes rather than content, or keeping customer data in retrieval indexes\n(which can be deleted) instead of in fine-tuned weights.","example":"A US hospital network wants an assistant that summarizes clinician notes. Requirements stated by the\ncustomer: HIPAA, the vendor's SOC 2 Type II, and their own policy of seven-year audit retention. The\ntranslation into constraints and controls:\n\n1. **BAA chain.** Notes are PHI, so the vendor signs a BAA with the hospital, and every subprocessor that\n   sees PHI must be covered too: the cloud provider and the model provider. The model provider's offering\n   is checked for BAA eligibility; a provider without one is excluded, whatever its quality.\n2. **Retention and training.** The model API path must not retain prompts beyond what the BAA allows and\n   must not train on them. Where that cannot be confirmed, the design switches to a self-hosted open-weight\n   model in the hospital's VPC.\n3. **Logs.** Debug traces originally captured full prompts. Under minimum-necessary handling, traces now keep\n   request IDs, token counts, latency and a hash of the input; full content is stored only in the hospital's\n   own audit store, with access logged.\n4. **Audit.** Seven-year retention applies to the record of who generated which summary and when, not to the\n   vendor's debug traces, which are kept for 30 days. Separating the two resolves the conflict between audit\n   and minimization.\n5. **Human oversight.** Summaries are drafts that a clinician must approve before they enter the record; the\n   approval event is part of the audit trail.\n6. **Evidence pack.** BAA list, data-flow diagram, retention configuration in IaC, access review records, and\n   the SOC 2 report, ready for the hospital's security review.\n\nThe result changed two design decisions (model provider selection and log content) before any code shipped.","tradeoffs":["Region-pinning and single-provider routing satisfy residency simply, but remove cross-region failover; if availability targets are strict, you need multiple in-region providers or accept degraded modes.","Self-hosting a model in the customer's environment satisfies the strictest data-custody constraints, but costs GPU capacity, operations and often model quality; API providers with suitable terms win when available.","Minimal logging lowers compliance exposure, but makes production debugging harder; structured metadata, hashes and on-demand, access-logged content capture recover most of the debugging value.","Building a single compliant tier for every customer simplifies operations, but over-serves smaller customers; separate tiers cost more to run and must be described precisely in questionnaires."],"pitfalls":["Treating \"compliant\" as a property of a vendor rather than of a configuration; the symptom is using a provider's HIPAA-eligible service without a signed BAA or with ineligible features enabled.","Forgetting derived data: embeddings, caches, eval datasets and traces contain the same personal data; the symptom is an erasure request completed in the database while vectors and logs still hold it.","Fine-tuning on customer personal data without a deletion story; the symptom is an erasure request that can only be honored by retraining or discarding the model.","Letting an engineering change silently violate a constraint, such as a new fallback region or a new analytics SDK; the symptom is an audit finding against a previously compliant system.","Deciding the EU AI Act risk tier or other legal classifications in engineering alone; the symptom is a design built for the wrong obligations, discovered when counsel finally reviews it."],"when":"Ask about compliance in discovery, before choosing a deployment model or model provider: which regimes\napply, which customer policies go beyond them, and what evidence their reviewers will want. Translate\neach into where/who/what/how-long/how/proof constraints, record them as non-functional requirements, and\nenforce them in code and configuration so evidence comes for free. Revisit them whenever data sources,\nregions, providers or features change. Do not attempt legal interpretation yourself: bring counsel in for\nclassification and contract terms. For the full design of controls, audit trails and residency, go to the\nsecurity-governance-compliance module. In interviews, show the translation from regime to architecture.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements constraints as code: region and provider allowlists in the gateway, log redaction, retention policies in IaC, and deletion that reaches embeddings and caches.","architect":"Maps each applicable regime to architectural constraints, resolves conflicts such as audit retention versus minimization, and chooses the deployment model and providers that satisfy them.","fde":"Elicits the customer's real obligations and internal policies early, confirms subprocessor agreements like BAAs, and assembles the evidence pack for their compliance and security teams."},"check":{"q":"An EU customer says \"we are GDPR-regulated, so everything must stay in the EU.\" Your system uses an\nEU-hosted model by default, a US-hosted fallback, and fine-tunes a small model on customer tickets. What\ndo you check and change?","a":"First separate the law from the policy: GDPR permits transfers with safeguards, but the customer's\ncontractual requirement is stricter and is what you must meet. The US fallback violates it, so disable\ncross-region fallback for this tenant and use a second EU-region provider or a degraded mode instead. Check\nevery subprocessor for EU processing, including tracing and error tools. Fine-tuning on tickets creates an\nerasure problem, because personal data in weights cannot be selectively removed; either drop fine-tuning in\nfavor of retrieval over deletable indexes, train only on anonymized data, or agree a retraining policy.\nEnsure deletion reaches embeddings and caches. Document it all in the DPA and data-flow diagram."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are compliance requirements best gathered before choosing the deployment model?","options":["Because auditors require requirements documents to be dated before code","Because constraints such as residency, subprocessor agreements and retention determine which regions, providers and deployment models are allowed","Because compliance requirements rarely change after launch","Because compliance reviews are faster for systems with fewer services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compliance rules are architectural: they can rule out a model provider, a region or SaaS hosting entirely. Learning them after the build forces rework, which is the cost early discovery avoids."},{"q":"A hospital asks whether your assistant may use a hosted model API for clinical notes. What is the deciding requirement under HIPAA?","options":["The model must score above a set threshold on medical benchmarks","The API must be hosted in the same state as the hospital","The prompts must be encrypted with the hospital's own keys","The model provider, as a subprocessor handling PHI, must be covered by a business associate agreement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PHI may flow to a vendor and its subprocessors only under BAAs. Encryption and hosting location matter for security, but without a BAA the flow is not permitted regardless of quality or location."},{"q":"An auditor requires seven-year retention of activity records while the privacy team demands minimal logging of personal data. Which design resolves both?","options":["Keep full prompts and outputs for seven years in the debug trace store","Disable logging entirely to satisfy minimization","Retain an audit record of who did what and when, with content in an access-controlled store, while debug traces keep only metadata for a short period","Encrypt the logs so retention rules no longer apply"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Separating audit records from debug telemetry satisfies each requirement on its own terms. Keeping all content for seven years violates minimization, encryption does not remove retention duties, and disabling logs fails the audit obligation."}]},{"key":"deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy, 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"An AI system going live in a customer environment touches many owners at once: the engineers who built\nthe prompt and retrieval pipeline, the customer's security team, their network and identity teams, the\nbusiness owner who defined success, and whoever will be paged at 3 a.m. Each group checks its own slice.\nNobody checks the seams: the eval suite passed, but on last month's index; the security review approved\nthe architecture, but not the new tool the agent gained afterwards; dashboards exist, but no alert routes\nto a human; a rollback plan exists, but nobody has run it.\n\nThe naive alternative is \"the tech lead decides we're ready\", usually under a date that was promised to\na sponsor. That fails for two reasons. First, one person cannot hold the full checklist in their head, and\nthe items they forget are precisely the ones outside their expertise. Second, schedule pressure biases\nthe decision: without criteria written down in advance, every gap gets rationalised as \"we'll fix it in\nhypercare\". The review exists to make the decision *structural*: criteria agreed before the date, evidence\ninstead of assertions, and an explicit record of which risks were accepted and by whom. Google's SRE\npractice formalised a version of this as the Production Readiness Review, run before an SRE team agrees\nto carry the pager for a service; the same logic applies when a customer's operations team is about to\ninherit your system.","how":"### 1. Agree the criteria early\nThe checklist is drafted at the start of the build, not the week before launch, because criteria written\nafter the system exists tend to describe what was built rather than what is needed. For an AI system the\ncategories are usually:\n\n- **Quality:** eval results on the golden set and customer-specific regression suite meet the acceptance\n  criteria agreed in scoping; UAT is signed off; known failure modes are documented.\n- **Safety and security:** security and architecture review approved for the *current* design; guardrails\n  tested against the red-team set; data handling (retention, PII, residency) matches the contract.\n- **Operability:** health checks, dashboards and alerts exist and route to a named on-call; every alert\n  links to a runbook; logs and traces are retained long enough to debug an incident.\n- **Capacity and cost:** load test at expected peak plus headroom; provider quotas and rate limits\n  confirmed in writing; per-request and daily cost ceilings enforced, not just estimated.\n- **Release mechanics:** rollout stages, promotion criteria and rollback triggers defined; rollback\n  rehearsed in a production-like environment; feature flag or kill switch verified.\n- **People:** support model and escalation paths agreed; customer team trained; hypercare staffed.\n\n### 2. Evidence, not assertions\nEach item has an owner and a link to evidence: an eval report with its dataset version, a load-test\nresult, a screenshot of an alert firing in staging, the ticket where security signed off. The rule is\n*because* \"done\" is the cheapest word to say and the most expensive to be wrong about. An item without\nevidence is treated as not done.\n\nFor AI systems, evidence must also be pinned to the configuration that is actually deploying. Record a\nfingerprint of the release (model identifier, prompt version or hash, retrieval config, index build,\ntool list) and attach it to every eval and load-test result, *because* an eval run on last week's prompt\nis evidence about a different system. If the fingerprint on the evidence does not match the release\ncandidate, the item reverts to not done, which is exactly how the \"passed, but on last month's index\" gap\nfrom above gets caught mechanically instead of by luck.\n\n### 3. Classify every item\nItems are **blocking** (no launch without them, e.g. rollback works, PII is not logged) or\n**non-blocking** (launch can proceed if a gap is accepted). A non-blocking gap becomes a **waiver**: a\nnamed risk owner, a compensating control, and an expiry date. Waivers expire *because* \"temporary\"\nexceptions without dates become permanent.\n\n### 4. Decide and record\nDecision rights are fixed before the meeting: who chairs, who decides, and which owners hold a veto on\ntheir blocking items (security on data handling, the operating team on operability). Writing this down\nmatters *because* a review where the most senior person present decides everything collapses back into\nthe \"tech lead decides\" failure, just with more attendees. The people who will carry the consequences,\nusually the team taking the pager and the business owner, must be among the deciders, not the audience.\n\nThe meeting is short when the prep is good: walk the open items, not the green ones. Outcomes are *go*,\n*conditional go* (launch with listed waivers), or *no-go* with the specific items that must change. The\ndecision and its waivers are written down, because when an incident happens in week two, the first\nquestion will be \"did we know about this?\".\n\n### 5. Re-run on material change\nA review approves a specific configuration: model version, prompt, tools, index, data sources. Swapping\nthe model or giving an agent a write-capable tool is a new launch for review purposes, scaled down to the\nitems the change touches.","example":"A readiness gate kept as data next to the code, so the go/no-go is computed rather than argued. The\nscenario: an insurer's claims-triage assistant, two weeks before go-live.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nitems = [  # (id, blocking, evidence_link_or_None, passed, waiver)\n    (\"eval.golden_set>=0.85\",   True,  \"evals/run-0412.json\", True,  None),\n    (\"security.review_current\", True,  \"SEC-2291\",            True,  None),\n    (\"rollback.rehearsed\",      True,  None,                  False, None),\n    (\"alerts.route_to_oncall\",  True,  \"staging-alert.png\",   True,  None),\n    (\"load.peak_x2\",            False, \"load/0409.html\",      False,\n        {\"owner\": \"cust-ops-lead\", \"control\": \"rate limit 20 rps\", \"expires\": date(2026, 11, 30)}),\n    (\"training.agents_trained\", False, None,                  False, None),\n]\n\ndef decide(items, today):\n    blockers, waived, gaps = [], [], []\n    for iid, blocking, evidence, passed, waiver in items:\n        ok = passed and evidence is not None          # no evidence means not done\n        if ok:\n            continue\n        if blocking:\n            blockers.append(iid)\n        elif waiver and waiver[\"expires\"] > today:\n            waived.append((iid, waiver[\"owner\"], waiver[\"expires\"]))\n        else:\n            gaps.append(iid)                            # non-blocking but unowned: still a no-go\n    if blockers or gaps:\n        return \"NO-GO\", blockers + gaps\n    return (\"CONDITIONAL GO\" if waived else \"GO\"), waived\n\nprint(decide(items, date(2026, 10, 20)))\n# ('NO-GO', ['rollback.rehearsed', 'training.agents_trained'])\n```\n\nRehearsing the rollback and either training the agents or writing a waiver for it turns this into a\nconditional go, with the load-test gap explicitly owned by the customer's ops lead until 30 November.","tradeoffs":["A formal review catches cross-team gaps, but adds calendar time; for a low-risk internal tool a lightweight checklist in the pull request is enough, and a full meeting is ceremony.","Strict blocking criteria prevent bad launches, but if everything is blocking the team learns to game evidence; keep the blocking list short and reserve it for irreversible or customer-visible harm.","Waivers let launches proceed under known risk, but each one is debt; the trade flips when waivers outnumber fixes across releases, which means the criteria or the schedule are wrong.","A central review board brings consistency across customers, but becomes a bottleneck at scale; mature teams move to self-service checklists with automated evidence and reserve humans for exceptions."],"pitfalls":["Running the review the day before launch, so every finding becomes a waiver; the symptom is a \"conditional go\" with more conditions than the previous three launches combined.","Accepting \"yes, done\" without a link; the symptom is an incident where the alert, runbook or rollback that was marked green turns out never to have existed.","Reviewing the architecture that was approved months ago rather than what is deploying now; the symptom is a tool, data source or model in production that no security reviewer ever saw.","Waivers with no expiry or owner; the symptom is the same accepted risk appearing in the next quarter's review with nobody able to say who agreed to it.","Treating AI quality as a single number; the symptom is a launch that passed the aggregate eval but fails a critical slice (one claim type, one language) that the business cares most about."],"when":"Hold one before any first production launch, before handing operations to a customer team, and before\nmaterial changes: a new model or provider, a new tool with side effects, a new data source, a new\nregion. Scale it to risk: a prompt tweak behind a flag needs an automated eval gate, not a meeting; an\nagent that can issue refunds needs the full review. Measure the review itself by what escapes it: track\nincidents in the first weeks after launch and ask whether a checklist item would have caught each one,\nthen add it. In interviews, a strong answer describes criteria, evidence, waivers with expiry, and the\nre-review trigger, not just \"we had a launch checklist\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the evidence: versioned eval reports, load-test results, a rehearsed rollback, and alerts proven to fire, linked from each checklist item rather than described.","architect":"Owns the criteria and the blocking list, decides which changes trigger re-review, and signs off on which risks the organisation is willing to accept with waivers.","fde":"Runs the review with the customer's security, ops and business owners in the room, so their sign-offs and accepted risks are recorded before the go-live date is defended."},"check":{"q":"Two days before go-live, the load test shows the system handles only 60% of forecast peak because\nthe customer's provider quota is lower than requested. The sponsor wants to launch. What do you do?","a":"First classify the gap: is overload customer-visible harm or graceful degradation? If the system\nhas a rate limiter and a clear \"busy, try again\" path, capacity can be non-blocking, so I would propose\na conditional go with a waiver: the customer's ops lead owns the risk, the compensating control is a\nrate limit plus a staged rollout capped below 60% of peak, and the waiver expires when the quota\nincrease lands. If overload would cause silent failures or timeouts mid-workflow, it is blocking and the\nanswer is no-go or a reduced launch scope. Either way the decision is written down with its owner."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most distinguishes a readiness review from a team simply agreeing they are ready?","options":["It is attended by more senior people","Each criterion is agreed in advance and judged against linked evidence with a named owner","It replaces the need for evals and load tests","It always happens after the first week of production traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The value is structural: criteria fixed before schedule pressure, evidence instead of assertions, and recorded ownership. Seniority alone does not prevent unchecked seams, and the review consumes evals rather than replacing them."},{"q":"A non-blocking item failed. What makes it acceptable to launch anyway?","options":["The tech lead believes it will be fixed during hypercare","The item is moved to the backlog with a low priority","A waiver with a named risk owner, a compensating control and an expiry date","The sponsor approves the launch date"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A waiver makes the accepted risk explicit and temporary. Backlog items and verbal assurances have no owner or deadline, so the gap silently becomes permanent and nobody is accountable when it bites."},{"q":"Which change most clearly requires re-running at least part of the readiness review?","options":["Rewording a help-text string in the UI","Rotating an API key on its normal schedule","Adding a new dashboard panel","Giving the agent a new tool that can write to the customer's CRM"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A review approves a specific configuration. A write-capable tool changes the security surface, the failure modes and the rollback story, so the affected items (security, guardrails, runbooks) must be re-checked."}]},{"key":"deployment/uat","sources":[],"why":"Engineering tests verify that the system behaves as the builders understood the requirement. They\ncannot catch a requirement that was misunderstood, a workflow step nobody mentioned, or an output that is\ntechnically correct but useless in context: a claims summary that omits the one field adjusters always\ncheck, an answer that cites the right policy but in a format the compliance team cannot file. Only the\npeople who will live with the system know those things, and they often only discover them by using it.\n\nUAT also has a contractual role. In enterprise and forward-deployed work, acceptance is often the\nmilestone that triggers payment or the move from pilot to production. Without a defined UAT, acceptance\nbecomes a negotiation over impressions: the sponsor saw two bad answers in a demo and the project\nstalls, or everyone says \"looks good\" and the system is abandoned three weeks after launch.\n\nThe naive version, \"give users access for two weeks and ask what they think\", fails for AI systems in\nparticular. Users try a handful of queries, judge on memorable failures, and each person's sample is too\nsmall to mean anything. Because model output varies run to run, the same question may pass for one\ntester and fail for another, and without a rubric the disagreement cannot be resolved. A useful UAT is\ndesigned like an experiment: fixed criteria, representative cases, a scoring rubric, a sample large\nenough to separate \"good enough\" from \"not quite\".","how":"### 1. Fix acceptance criteria before testing starts\nCriteria come from scoping and are written as measurable statements: \"at least 85% of triage\nrecommendations rated correct or acceptable by adjusters\", \"zero responses that disclose another\ncustomer's data\", \"p95 response under 8 seconds\". Splitting criteria into **hard gates** (safety,\nprivacy, legal: any failure blocks) and **rate thresholds** (quality metrics judged on a sample) matters\n*because* a single privacy leak is not averaged away by 99 good answers.\n\n### 2. Build a representative case set\nDraw cases from real historical work, stratified by the slices the business cares about (claim type,\nlanguage, document quality, customer tier), plus deliberately chosen edge cases. Representativeness\nmatters *because* the pass rate on easy cases tells you nothing about the hard ones. Keep each case's\nexpected outcome or reference answer, written by subject-matter experts, so scoring is not improvised.\n\n### 3. Run in a production-like environment\nUAT runs against the real integrations (identity, document stores, downstream systems) with\nproduction-shaped data, in an environment isolated from production writes. Running on mocks would hide\nthe integration failures that UAT is best at catching: permission errors, slow connectors, malformed\ndocuments.\n\n### 4. Score with a rubric, not a thumbs-up\nTesters rate each output on a small scale with written definitions (for example *correct*, *acceptable\nwith minor edit*, *wrong*, *harmful*). Overlap a fraction of cases between testers and measure agreement,\n*because* if two adjusters disagree on the same output the criterion itself is ambiguous and must be\nfixed before the numbers mean anything.\n\n### 5. Size the sample and handle non-determinism\nA rate threshold is a claim about the true pass rate, so the sample must be big enough that the lower\nend of its confidence interval clears the bar. A quick planning estimate uses the normal approximation:\n\n```\nn ≈ z² · p(1 − p) / m²        z = 1.96 for 95%, p = expected pass rate, m = margin you can afford\n```\n\nIf you expect about 88% against an 85% bar, the margin is 3 points: 1.96² × 0.88 × 0.12 / 0.03² ≈ 450\ncases. The Wilson interval used to report the result is slightly more conservative and needs roughly 550.\nThe lesson of the formula is the *m²* in the denominator: halving the margin quadruples the cases, which\nis why a criterion set just below the expected rate is expensive to prove, and why negotiating the bar\nand the sample together during scoping is cheaper than discovering the problem at sign-off.\n\nNon-determinism changes what counts as a sample. Running each case three times does not turn 200 cases\ninto 600 independent observations, *because* runs of the same case share the same input and difficulty\nand fail together. Score at the case level (for example \"passes on at least 2 of 3 runs\", or the\nper-case pass fraction averaged across cases), and report the run-to-run flip rate separately: a case\nthat passes once and fails once is a stability defect in its own right, even if the average looks fine.\n\n### 6. Triage defects by cause\nEvery failure gets a severity (blocker, major, minor) and a cause: **integration** (wrong data reached the\nmodel), **retrieval** (right data existed, was not found), **model/prompt** (right context, wrong answer),\nor **requirement** (system did what was specified, specification was wrong). The cause decides the fix and\nwhether a re-test is needed. A \"requirement\" finding is a change request, not a defect, and treating it as\na defect is how UAT never ends.\n\n### 7. Exit and sign off\nUAT exits when hard gates are clean, rate thresholds are met with enough confidence, and no open blockers\nremain. Sign-off is a named person on the customer side, recorded. Every failing case found during UAT\ngoes into the customer-specific regression suite so it stays fixed.","example":"An insurer's acceptance criterion is \"at least 85% of triage recommendations rated correct or\nacceptable\". Adjusters score 200 stratified cases and 176 pass: **88%**. The project manager wants to\nsign off. The question is whether 88% on 200 cases is evidence that the true rate is at least 85%.\n\nA 95% Wilson interval for 176/200 is roughly **82.8% to 91.8%**. The lower bound is below 85%, so this\nsample cannot distinguish \"comfortably above the bar\" from \"slightly below it\". With the same 88% rate,\n400 cases give roughly 84.4% to 90.8% (still not clear), and about 600 cases give roughly 85.2% to 90.4%,\nwhich clears it. So the options are concrete: score about 400 more cases, renegotiate the criterion to be\njudged on the point estimate (an explicit, recorded decision), or improve the system so the observed rate\nrises.\n\nThe slice view matters too. Of the 24 failures, 15 are on handwritten claim forms, which are 20% of cases\n(40 of 200). That slice passes only 25 of 40, about 62%, while typed forms pass 151 of 160, about 94%.\nThe aggregate hides that the system is not fit for handwritten claims. Triage shows 11 of the 15 are\nOCR-quality failures (integration), not model reasoning, so the fix is the document parser, followed by a\nre-test of that slice only.","tradeoffs":["Larger, stratified case sets give statistically meaningful results, but consume scarce SME time; when experts are expensive, spend the budget on the high-risk slices and accept wider intervals elsewhere.","Scripted test cases make results comparable and repeatable, while exploratory use surfaces unknown workflow needs; run both, and weight exploratory findings more heavily early in UAT.","Strict exit criteria protect the customer, but may block a system that is already better than the current manual process; when a baseline exists, judging against it instead of an absolute bar can be the more honest criterion.","Testing on production-like data catches real integration failures, but requires data access approvals that can take weeks; start those approvals at kickoff, not at UAT."],"pitfalls":["Defining acceptance criteria during UAT rather than before; the symptom is a moving target where each round of fixes surfaces a new expectation nobody wrote down.","Letting the build team score outputs instead of the customer's users; the symptom is a high UAT pass rate followed by low adoption and complaints in the first week of hypercare.","Running each case once and treating the result as fixed; the symptom is testers reporting a failure that the engineers \"cannot reproduce\" because a re-run happens to pass.","Treating every change request as a defect; the symptom is UAT rounds that never converge and a backlog where scope creep is indistinguishable from bugs.","Judging on the aggregate only; the symptom is a passing headline number while one important slice, such as one language or document type, fails badly."],"when":"Run UAT whenever a system will be used by people other than its builders and acceptance carries weight:\na customer go-live, a pilot-to-production gate, a contractual milestone. Plan it during scoping, because\nthe case set, rubric, environment and data approvals take longer than the testing itself. For internal\ntools with a small user group, a lighter version (a few power users, a short rubric) is enough. Measure\npass rate with a confidence interval, per-slice pass rates, inter-rater agreement and defect counts by\ncause. In interviews, the depth signal is talking about criteria fixed in advance, sample size,\nnon-determinism and triage by cause, rather than \"users tried it and liked it\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the UAT harness: case loading, repeated runs per case, rubric capture, and export of every failure into the regression suite with its trace attached.","architect":"Defines the UAT environment boundary, ensuring production-like integrations and data without production writes, and owns how acceptance criteria map to measurable signals.","fde":"Recruits the customer's testers, runs calibration on the rubric, triages findings with them, and separates genuine defects from change requests in front of the sponsor."},"check":{"q":"UAT testers rate a summarisation feature at 90% acceptable, but two senior adjusters say it is\nunusable. How do you reconcile this before sign-off?","a":"First check whether the disagreement is about the rubric or the cases. Have the seniors and other\ntesters score the same overlapping outputs; low agreement means \"acceptable\" is defined differently and\nthe rubric needs tightening, perhaps because seniors need a field others ignore. Then check slices: the\nseniors may handle complex claims that are under-represented in the case set. If so, stratify, add cases\nfrom their workload and report per-slice rates. If their concern is a workflow need never in the\ncriteria, record it as a change request with the sponsor rather than a defect, and decide whether it\nblocks."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why split UAT criteria into hard gates and rate thresholds?","options":["Hard gates are tested by engineers and rate thresholds by users","Some failures, like a privacy leak, are unacceptable even once, so they cannot be averaged into a pass rate","Rate thresholds only apply to latency, not quality","Hard gates make UAT shorter by skipping sampling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A pass rate averages over cases, which is right for quality but wrong for safety, privacy or legal failures where a single occurrence is a blocker regardless of how many other cases succeed."},{"q":"UAT shows 88% pass on 200 cases against an 85% bar. What is the most defensible conclusion?","options":["The system passes because 88% exceeds 85%","The system fails because some cases failed","The sample is too small to show the true rate clears 85%, so either test more cases or explicitly agree to judge on the point estimate","The result is invalid because AI outputs are non-deterministic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The 95% interval for 176 of 200 reaches below 85%, so the data is consistent with being under the bar. Non-determinism does not invalidate sampling; it is one more reason to treat the rate statistically."},{"q":"A tester reports that the system correctly followed the specification, but the specified output format does not fit their filing process. How should it be classified?","options":["As a blocker defect in the model","As a retrieval failure","As a minor integration bug","As a requirement finding, handled as a change request with the sponsor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The system did what was specified; the specification was wrong or incomplete. Treating it as a defect hides scope change and stops UAT from converging, while a change request makes the cost explicit."}]},{"key":"deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","sources":[],"why":"A staged rollout only reduces risk if someone decides, at each stage, whether to continue. Without\nwritten criteria that decision is made by whoever is watching the dashboard, at the moment the numbers\nare ambiguous, with a launch date behind them. The predictable failure is \"it's probably noise, let's\nkeep going\", followed by an incident at 100% that was visible at 5%. The opposite failure also happens:\nan engineer rolls back on a spike that was pure noise, and the team loses confidence in releasing at all.\n\nAI releases make this harder in three ways. First, the thing being released is often not code: a new\nprompt, a different model version, a re-embedded index or a changed retrieval parameter. These can\ndegrade quality with no rise in errors or latency at all. Second, quality signals are slow and noisy:\nthumbs-down rates, escalations to humans and LLM-judge scores on sampled traffic need hundreds of\nrequests and hours or days before a difference is distinguishable from noise. Third, some AI actions are\nirreversible. An agent that sent emails or updated records during a bad rollout cannot be rolled back by\nredeploying the old version.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"watch the dashboards and use judgement\", fails because judgement under pressure\nis biased toward continuing. Writing the criteria in advance moves the hard thinking to a calm moment and\nmakes the in-the-moment decision mechanical.","how":"### 1. Define the stages and what each must prove\nA typical ladder is internal users, then a small percentage or one friendly tenant, then a larger\nfraction, then everyone. Each stage has a **minimum duration** and a **minimum sample** (requests,\nsessions or completed tasks). Duration matters *because* traffic varies by hour and weekday; a stage that\nonly saw Sunday-night traffic proved little about Monday morning. Sample size matters *because* rare\nfailures cannot be observed in small samples. Error counts are roughly Poisson, so with *n* requests at\nbaseline rate *p* you expect *np* errors with a standard deviation of about √(np). At a 1% baseline, 100\nrequests give about 1 error (±1), so a canary at 2% is indistinguishable from noise; 3,000 requests give\nabout 30 (±5.5) for the old rate against about 60 for a doubled one, a gap of more than five standard\ndeviations. The minimum sample is therefore derived from the baseline rate and the smallest regression\nyou care about, not picked as a round number.\n\n### Quality signals need far bigger samples\nThe same arithmetic is harsher for quality, because the regressions that matter are a few points on a\nrate near 90%, not a doubling. To detect a drop in LLM-judge pass rate from 91% to 88% with the usual\nsignificance (5%) and power (80%), the two-proportion sample size is\n\n```\nn per arm ≈ (z_α/2 + z_β)² · (p₁q₁ + p₂q₂) / (p₁ − p₂)²\n          = (1.96 + 0.84)² · (0.91·0.09 + 0.88·0.12) / 0.03² ≈ 1,600 judged responses\n```\n\nThat is judged responses, not requests: if you judge 10% of traffic, the stage needs about 16,000\nrequests before a 3-point quality drop is reliably visible. This is why quality criteria force longer\nbake times than error criteria, and why a stage that has \"enough requests\" may still have too few\njudgements to decide anything about quality.\n\n### 2. Compare against a baseline, not an absolute\nWhere possible run the old and new version side by side on comparable traffic, so seasonal or upstream\neffects hit both. Criteria are expressed as relative regressions (\"canary p95 latency no more than 20%\nabove control\") plus absolute floors so tiny baselines do not produce absurd ratios.\n\n### 3. Split triggers into hard and soft\n- **Hard triggers** are automated and immediate: error rate, timeouts, p95 or p99 latency, guardrail\n  block rate, cost per request, provider 429s, crash loops. They are fast to measure and unambiguous.\n- **Soft triggers** need a human decision within a set time: online quality score from an LLM judge on\n  sampled traffic, user feedback rate, escalation-to-human rate, task completion rate. They are slower and\n  noisier, so they set a *hold* rather than an automatic rollback, and a named person decides.\n\n### 4. Make the outcome one of three states\nAt each evaluation the release is **promote** (all criteria met, duration and sample reached),\n**hold** (not enough evidence yet, or a soft trigger needs review) or **roll back** (any hard trigger\ntripped after the minimum sample). The explicit hold state prevents both premature promotion and panic\nrollbacks.\n\n### 5. Make rollback cheap and rehearsed\nVersion every artefact that changes behaviour: prompt, model identifier (pinned, never a floating alias),\nretrieval config, index. Ship behind a flag so rollback is a config change, not a redeploy. For index\nchanges keep the old index serving until the new one has baked, because re-embedding a corpus can take\nhours and is not a rollback you can do mid-incident. For agents with side effects, gate write actions\nbehind approval or dry-run during early stages, because no trigger can un-send an email.\n\n### 6. Decide roll forward versus roll back\nRolling back is the default because it restores a known state. Rolling forward with a fix is right only\nwhen rollback is impossible (a schema migration already applied, an external contract already changed)\nor when the old version is itself broken by an external change, such as a deprecated provider model.","example":"A stage gate evaluated every 15 minutes during a canary of a new prompt and model version. The numbers\nare illustrative thresholds a team might agree, not recommendations.\n\n```python\ndef evaluate(canary, control, minutes_in_stage, min_minutes=120, min_requests=3000, min_judged=1600):\n    # Hard triggers: checked as soon as there is enough data to trust them.\n    if canary[\"requests\"] >= min_requests:\n        err_limit = max(2 * control[\"error_rate\"], 0.01)          # relative, with an absolute floor\n        if canary[\"error_rate\"] > err_limit:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"error rate\"\n        if canary[\"p95_ms\"] > 1.2 * control[\"p95_ms\"]:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"p95 latency\"\n        if canary[\"cost_per_req\"] > 1.3 * control[\"cost_per_req\"]:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"cost per request\"\n        if canary[\"guardrail_block_rate\"] > control[\"guardrail_block_rate\"] + 0.02:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"guardrail blocks\"\n    # Not enough evidence yet: neither promote nor roll back.\n    if (canary[\"requests\"] < min_requests or minutes_in_stage < min_minutes\n            or canary[\"judged\"] < min_judged):\n        return \"HOLD\", \"collecting evidence\"\n    # Soft trigger: sampled LLM-judge quality needs a human if it drops.\n    if canary[\"judge_pass\"] < control[\"judge_pass\"] - 0.03:\n        return \"HOLD\", \"quality drop: page release owner\"\n    return \"PROMOTE\", \"all criteria met\"\n\ncontrol = {\"error_rate\": 0.004, \"p95_ms\": 4100, \"cost_per_req\": 0.011,\n           \"guardrail_block_rate\": 0.006, \"judge_pass\": 0.91, \"judged\": 5000}\ncanary  = {\"requests\": 18000, \"judged\": 1800, \"error_rate\": 0.005, \"p95_ms\": 4500, \"cost_per_req\": 0.012,\n           \"guardrail_block_rate\": 0.007, \"judge_pass\": 0.86}\nprint(evaluate(canary, control, minutes_in_stage=240))\n# ('HOLD', 'quality drop: page release owner')\n```\n\nErrors, latency (4500 is under 1.2 × 4100 = 4920) and cost are within limits, so no hard trigger fires,\nbut the sampled judge pass rate fell five points. With 1,800 judged canary responses (10% of 18,000\nrequests) against 5,000 on control, the standard error of the difference is just under one point, so a\nfive-point drop is more than five standard errors: very unlikely to be noise. The gate still holds and pages a human rather than rolling back automatically,\n*because* a judge-score drop can also mean the judge itself misreads the new model's style (for example\nshorter answers it penalises). The human reads a sample of failing judgements and decides. Had the same\ndrop appeared after only 200 judgements, the gate would have returned \"collecting evidence\", which is the\npoint of the judged-sample floor.","tradeoffs":["Long bake times and large minimum samples make decisions trustworthy, but slow down every release; the balance flips toward speed for low-risk changes behind flags and toward caution for model swaps.","Automated rollback reacts in minutes, but can thrash on noisy metrics; reserve automation for hard, low-noise signals and route noisy quality signals to a human hold.","Relative thresholds against a live control absorb traffic shifts, but require running two versions at once, which doubles some costs and is awkward for stateful or per-tenant releases.","Rolling out by tenant gives clean blast-radius control and clearer attribution, but small tenants produce too little traffic to judge, so per-tenant stages need longer durations."],"pitfalls":["Writing criteria after the rollout starts; the symptom is thresholds that conveniently sit just above whatever the canary is currently showing.","Judging a stage before its minimum sample; the symptom is releases promoted on a quiet hour and incidents that start when peak traffic arrives.","Monitoring only errors and latency for a prompt or model change; the symptom is a green rollout followed by a week of user complaints about worse answers.","Pointing at a floating model alias, so the provider changes the model underneath you; the symptom is a quality shift that no release on your side explains and that rollback does not fix.","Assuming rollback undoes everything; the symptom is a clean revert while records written or messages sent by the bad version remain in customer systems."],"when":"Use explicit criteria and triggers for any change that can alter behaviour in production: code, prompt,\nmodel, retrieval configuration, index, guardrail policy, tool set. Scale the ladder to risk: a copy change\nbehind a flag may need only a short internal stage; a model swap for an agent with write tools needs\nstaged tenants, a long bake and human-reviewed quality gates. Measure what the customer feels (errors,\nlatency, cost, task success) and what predicts it (judge score on sampled traffic, escalations). In\ninterviews, strong answers name the three-state decision, minimum samples, relative-plus-absolute\nthresholds, hard versus soft triggers, and the irreversibility of agent side effects.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the gate logic, version pinning and flags, and wires metrics so the canary and control are comparable on the same dashboard and alert rules.","architect":"Decides which artefacts are versioned and independently rollbackable, which signals are hard triggers, and how irreversible actions are fenced during early rollout stages.","fde":"Agrees the stages and thresholds with the customer before go-live, including who on their side can call a rollback and how affected users are told."},"check":{"q":"A new model version is at 10% of traffic. Error rate and latency are fine, but the thumbs-down\nrate is 4.1% versus 3.2% on control after 900 rated responses. Roll back, hold or promote?","a":"Hold, not promote, and probably not an automatic rollback yet. Thumbs-down is a noisy soft\nsignal: 900 ratings at 4.1% is about 37 negatives against about 29 expected at the control rate. The\nstandard error of a rate near 4% on 900 ratings is roughly 0.65 points (more once the control's own\nnoise is added), so the 0.9-point gap is at most about 1.4 standard errors, well within chance, before even counting bias from which users choose to rate. I would extend the stage until the sample\nis large enough, read a sample of the negative responses to see whether they share a cause, and run the\nLLM judge on matched traffic for a less biased signal. If the failures cluster on a real regression,\nroll back; if they are spread and the gap closes with more data, continue."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should rollback triggers be written before the rollout begins?","options":["So they can be loaded into the deployment tool's configuration file","Because decisions made while watching ambiguous live numbers are biased toward continuing the release","Because thresholds cannot be changed once traffic is flowing","Because auditors require a document for every deployment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pre-committed thresholds move the reasoning to a calm moment. In the moment, sunk cost and schedule pressure make \"probably noise\" the default reading of a bad number, which is how 5% problems reach 100%."},{"q":"Which signal is the best candidate for an automated, immediate rollback trigger?","options":["Average user satisfaction rating from in-app surveys","LLM-judge quality score on 50 sampled responses","Error rate exceeding twice the control rate after the minimum request count","Number of support tickets opened this week"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Error rate is fast, low-noise and unambiguous once a minimum sample exists. Survey ratings, small judge samples and tickets are slow or noisy, so they should trigger a human-reviewed hold rather than automation."},{"q":"A rollout re-embedded the whole corpus with a new embedding model. What makes rollback safe?","options":["Re-running the old embedding model over the corpus if quality drops","Pinning the new embedding model version","Rolling back the application code only","Keeping the old index serving in parallel until the new one has passed its bake period"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Re-embedding takes hours, so it is not a mid-incident rollback. Keeping the old index live lets rollback be a configuration switch, while code-only rollback would query vectors from an incompatible model."}]},{"key":"deployment/runbooks","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy, 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"When something breaks, the knowledge needed to fix it usually lives in a few engineers' heads: which\ndashboard shows the real problem, which flag switches to the fallback model, which log query finds the\nfailing tenant. That works until those engineers are asleep, on holiday, or have moved to another\nproject. For forward-deployed work it fails structurally: after handoff, the customer's operations team\nis on call for a system they did not build, often in an environment the vendor cannot access.\n\nThe naive alternatives both fail. \"The on-call will figure it out\" turns every incident into an\ninvestigation, stretching time to mitigation from minutes to hours while users are affected. A long\narchitecture document does not help either: it explains how the system works, not what to do when the\nprovider is returning 429s, and nobody reads twenty pages during an outage.\n\nAI systems add failure modes that conventional operations teams have not seen: an LLM provider degrading\nor rate-limiting, a cost spike from a prompt loop or an abusive user, a sudden rise in guardrail blocks,\na stale or partially rebuilt retrieval index, a jailbreak or prompt-injection report, a model answering\nconfidently and wrongly after an upstream change. Each needs a specific response, and some (pausing an\nagent's write tools, notifying security) carry business consequences the on-call needs explicit\npermission to take. A runbook is where that permission and procedure are written down in advance.","how":"### One situation per runbook\nA runbook covers one alert or symptom, not a whole service, *because* the reader arrives from a specific\npage and needs the steps for that situation, not a tour. Every alert links to its runbook; an alert with\nno runbook is a sign the alert is either unnecessary or the knowledge is missing.\n\n### A fixed structure\n1. **Trigger:** the alert name and threshold, or the user-reported symptom.\n2. **Impact:** who is affected and how badly, with a severity guide, *because* severity decides who is\n   woken up and how fast customers are told.\n3. **Confirm:** one or two checks that tell a real problem from a false alarm, with exact dashboard\n   links or queries.\n4. **Mitigate:** the steps that stop user harm, in order, with exact commands or flag names. Mitigation\n   comes before diagnosis *because* restoring service is urgent and root cause can wait for the\n   postmortem.\n5. **Verify:** what metric must return to normal, and for how long, before declaring recovery.\n6. **Escalate:** who to call if mitigation fails or the situation does not match, with names or rotas,\n   not \"the platform team\".\n7. **Afterwards:** what to record for the postmortem (timeline, traces, request IDs).\n\n### Precision over prose\nSteps are imperative and copy-pasteable: the flag name, the query, the expected output. Decision points\nare explicit (\"if 429s are from one tenant, go to step 6; otherwise continue\"). Vague steps such as\n\"check the logs\" are where incidents stall.\n\n### Pre-authorised actions\nRunbooks state which disruptive actions the on-call may take without asking: switching to a fallback\nmodel, disabling an agent's write tools, blocking a tenant, shedding load. Pre-authorisation exists\n*because* waiting for a manager's approval at night extends the incident.\n\n### Automation is the next step, not a replacement\nA runbook step that is run identically every time is a candidate for a script, then for automatic\nremediation. Keep the runbook even after automation, describing what the automation does and what to do\nwhen it fails, *because* automation fails in exactly the unusual situations where humans need context.\n\n### Store them where the outage is not\nA runbook is useless if it is unreachable during the incident it covers. Keep runbooks somewhere that\ndoes not share fate with the system: not only on a wiki behind the same single sign-on that may be\nfailing, not only in a cloud account the customer's on-call cannot access, and with an offline or mirrored\ncopy for the scenarios (identity outage, network partition into an air-gapped site) where the primary\nlocation goes down too. The alert should carry the link, *because* searching for the right page costs\nminutes the incident does not have.\n\nTerminology varies: some teams say *playbook* for the broader incident-handling process (roles,\ncommunication, severity) and *runbook* for the per-situation procedure described here. The distinction\nmatters less than having both, and not expecting one to do the other's job.\n\n### Keep them alive\nEach runbook has an owner and a last-tested date. Teams rehearse them in game days (deliberately\ninjected failures in staging) and update them after every incident where the runbook was wrong or\nmissing. A runbook that has never been executed should be assumed not to work.","example":"An abridged runbook for an LLM-backed assistant, written for a customer operations team after handoff.\nFlag names, dashboards and thresholds are illustrative stand-ins for the team's own.\n\n```markdown\n### RB-07 LLM provider degraded (429s / 5xx / timeouts)\nTrigger: alert `llm_upstream_error_rate > 5% for 5 min` (any tenant)\nImpact: answers fail or slow down for all users. SEV2; SEV1 if > 25% for 15 min.\n\nConfirm\n1. Dashboard \"LLM upstream\" -> panel \"errors by status code\".\n   429 = rate limit, 5xx/timeout = provider outage. Check the provider status page.\n2. Panel \"requests by tenant\": is one tenant > 50% of traffic? If yes, go to step 6.\n\nMitigate (pre-authorised, no approval needed)\n3. 5xx/timeouts: set flag `llm.route=fallback` (secondary provider/region). Expect\n   slightly lower answer quality; banner `degraded_mode=on` informs users.\n4. 429s, no single hot tenant: set `llm.max_concurrency=40` (default 80) to stop retry storms.\n5. Pause batch jobs: set `jobs.summarise_nightly.enabled=false`.\n6. One tenant flooding: set `tenant.<id>.rate_limit_rps=2`; notify their admin (template T-3).\n\nVerify\n7. Upstream error rate < 1% and p95 < 8 s for 15 min. Then revert step 3 first, watch 15 min,\n   then restore concurrency, then re-enable batch jobs last.\n\nEscalate\n8. Not recovered 30 min after step 3, or fallback also failing: page vendor on-call\n   (rota \"assistant-l3\"), open a ticket with the provider quoting request IDs from the trace.\nAfterwards: export the incident timeline and five failing trace IDs to the postmortem doc.\n```\n\nNotice what it encodes: a split between rate-limit and outage causes, a hot-tenant branch, actions the\noperator may take without approval, an explicit recovery condition, and an order for reverting changes.\nThe two branches exist *because* the same alert has opposite fixes: for a provider outage you move traffic\naway, while for rate limiting you reduce your own pressure, and failing over a rate-limited workload can\nsimply exhaust the fallback's quota too. The revert order is deliberate as well: one change at a time,\nwith a watch period, so that if errors return you know which reverted change they depended on, and the\nmost load-generating change (batch jobs) comes back last, when the system has proven it is stable.","tradeoffs":["Detailed, command-level runbooks make incidents fast for unfamiliar operators, but go stale quickly as the system changes; the cost flips when nobody owns updates, and a wrong runbook is worse than none.","Pre-authorising disruptive actions shortens incidents, but risks over-reaction on false alarms; keep the confirm step strict so disruptive mitigation follows evidence.","Automating remediation removes human delay, but hides the system's behaviour from operators; keep humans in the loop for actions with customer-visible or irreversible effects.","Many narrow runbooks match alerts precisely, but become hard to navigate; a short index by symptom helps when the triggering alert is not obvious."],"pitfalls":["Writing runbooks that explain the architecture instead of giving steps; the symptom is an on-call who read the page and still had to page the original engineer.","Never testing them; the symptom is a step that references a flag, dashboard or command that was renamed months ago, discovered mid-incident.","Alerts with no linked runbook; the symptom is repeated pages that each get the same ad-hoc response and no consistent mitigation time.","Leaving out AI-specific situations such as cost spikes, guardrail-block surges or prompt-injection reports; the symptom is a generic \"service degraded\" response to a problem that needed a kill switch.","Keeping runbooks only behind the system's own login or on infrastructure that fails with it; the symptom is an identity or network outage in which the on-call cannot open the page that tells them what to do.","Writing them only for the vendor team; the symptom is a customer operations team that escalates every page during hypercare because the runbooks assume access and context they do not have."],"when":"Write a runbook for every alert that pages a human and for every known failure mode a different team\nwill handle, before go-live and as a readiness-review item. Start with the situations most likely to\nhappen and most harmful when they do: provider outage, rate limits, cost runaway, index staleness, unsafe\noutput reports, authentication failures to customer systems. Measure them by time to mitigate, by how\noften responders escalate despite the runbook, and by how often an incident finds a runbook wrong. In\ninterviews, describing alert-linked runbooks with pre-authorised mitigations and rehearsal shows\noperational maturity beyond \"we have monitoring\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes runbooks for the failure modes they built in, with exact flags and queries, and adds a kill switch or fallback path wherever a runbook needs one that does not exist.","architect":"Ensures the system exposes the levers runbooks need (fallback routing, per-tenant limits, tool disablement) and defines which actions are pre-authorised for on-call.","fde":"Rewrites runbooks for the customer's operators, in their tools and access model, and runs a game day with them before handoff so the procedures are proven in their environment."},"check":{"q":"During hypercare the customer's on-call escalated every one of six incidents to your team, even\nthough runbooks existed. What would you investigate and change?","a":"I would review each incident with the on-call to see where they stopped. Common causes: the\nrunbook assumed access they lack (a vendor dashboard or cloud account); steps were vague (\"check the\nmodel\") instead of exact; the confirm step did not distinguish their situation so they did not trust the\nmitigation; or disruptive actions were not explicitly pre-authorised, so they waited for permission. Fixes\nfollow from the cause: rewrite with their tools and links, add decision branches, state pre-authorisation\nin writing with their management's agreement, then run a game day so they execute each runbook once\nunder realistic conditions."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do runbooks put mitigation steps before diagnosis?","options":["Diagnosis is the vendor's responsibility, not the on-call's","Stopping user harm is urgent, while root cause can be found afterwards from preserved evidence","Diagnosis cannot be written down in advance","Mitigation steps are easier to automate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The incident's cost accrues while users are affected, so restoring service comes first. Recording timelines and trace IDs preserves what the postmortem needs, so diagnosis is deferred, not lost."},{"q":"Which runbook step is most likely to stall an incident?","options":["\"Set flag llm.route=fallback\"","\"If one tenant exceeds 50% of traffic, go to step 6\"","\"Check the logs for anything unusual\"","\"Verify upstream error rate stays below 1% for 15 minutes\""],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vague instructions force the responder to reason from scratch, which is what runbooks exist to avoid. The other steps are exact actions, explicit branches or measurable recovery conditions."},{"q":"A team automated a runbook's mitigation as an auto-remediation job. What should happen to the runbook?","options":["Delete it, since the automation now handles the situation","Freeze it as historical documentation","Keep it, describing what the automation does and what to do if the automation fails or does not fire","Convert it into a postmortem template"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Automation fails in unusual conditions, which is when operators most need context. The runbook becomes the fallback for the automation and explains its behaviour so responders do not fight it."}]},{"key":"deployment/health-checks","sources":["Official docs: Kubernetes (liveness, readiness and startup probes)"],"why":"A load balancer or orchestrator cannot tell a healthy instance from a broken one unless the instance\ntells it. Without checks, traffic keeps flowing to a process that has deadlocked, run out of GPU memory,\nor is still loading a multi-gigabyte model, and users see errors while every infrastructure dashboard\nshows \"running\". Rolling deployments also depend on them: a new version is only safe to shift traffic to\nonce it says it is ready.\n\nThe naive check is a single `/health` endpoint that returns 200 if the web server responds. It fails in\nboth directions. It is too shallow, because a server can answer HTTP while its model failed to load or\nits connection pool is exhausted. And when teams make it deeper by checking every dependency (database,\nvector store, LLM provider) and wire it to the restart probe, it becomes dangerous: if the shared LLM\nprovider has a blip, every instance fails its check at once, the orchestrator restarts all of them, and\na brief upstream problem becomes a fleet-wide restart storm with cold model loads on top.\n\nThe fix is to recognise that \"healthy\" is not one question. Whether to restart, whether to route traffic\nand whether the product works end to end have different answers and different consequences, so each\ngets its own check with its own scope.","how":"### Liveness: should this process be restarted?\nLiveness answers only \"is this process irrecoverably stuck?\". It should check things a restart would fix:\nthe event loop responds, a worker thread is not deadlocked. It must **not** check external dependencies,\n*because* restarting your service does not fix someone else's outage and restarts at fleet scale add\nload exactly when the system is weakest. In Kubernetes, repeated liveness failures make the kubelet\nrestart the container.\n\n### Readiness: should this instance receive traffic right now?\nReadiness answers \"can I serve a request successfully at this moment?\". It checks local conditions: the\nmodel is loaded, the GPU is usable, the instance is not overloaded, the instance is not shutting down. In\nKubernetes a failing readiness probe removes the pod from the Service's endpoints without restarting it,\nso traffic flows to other replicas. Two design choices matter here:\n- **Shared dependencies are risky in readiness.** If every replica checks the same LLM provider and it\n  fails, every replica goes unready and the service has zero endpoints, so even a fallback path cannot be\n  served. Prefer to keep serving and degrade (fallback model, cached answer, clear error) and report the\n  dependency's state through metrics and alerts instead.\n- **Drain before exiting.** On shutdown the instance must stop receiving new requests, then finish\n  in-flight ones, *because* long LLM streams cut mid-answer are user-visible errors during every deploy.\n  Behind a plain load balancer that means failing readiness first and waiting before exit. Kubernetes\n  removes a terminating pod from endpoints itself, but asynchronously, so a short pre-stop delay plus a\n  graceful-shutdown timeout longer than the longest stream is the usual pattern.\n\n### Startup: has slow initialisation finished?\nLoading model weights, warming a GPU or building an in-memory index can take minutes. A startup probe\ncovers that window; in Kubernetes, liveness and readiness probes are not run until the startup probe\nsucceeds. Without it, teams either set a huge liveness delay (slow to detect real hangs later) or watch\npods get killed mid-load in a restart loop.\n\n### Synthetic end-to-end checks\nProbes tell infrastructure about one instance. They do not tell you the product works. A synthetic check\nruns from outside on a schedule: send a fixed golden request through the real entry point (auth,\nretrieval, model, guardrails) and assert on the answer, for example that it cites the expected document.\nThis catches failures probes never see: a stale index, an expired credential to a customer system, a\nmodel silently changed behind an alias. Synthetic results feed alerts and dashboards, not restarts.\n\n### Probe timing is arithmetic, not taste\nEach probe acts only after `failureThreshold` consecutive failures, checked every `periodSeconds`, so\nthe time before the platform reacts is roughly\n\n```\nreaction_time ≈ periodSeconds × failureThreshold   (+ up to timeoutSeconds per attempt)\n```\n\nThat one product sets three budgets. For **startup**, it must exceed the worst-case initialisation, not\nthe typical one: if weights load in 90 seconds normally but 4 minutes on a cold node pulling from object\nstorage, `periodSeconds: 10, failureThreshold: 30` gives a 300-second window, while a 60-second window\nwould kill every pod on a cold node and loop forever. For **liveness**, it is how long a hung process\nkeeps receiving its share of traffic before a restart, so a short product detects hangs fast but also\nrestarts pods that were merely slow under a load spike (a long GC pause, a CPU-starved event loop). For\n**readiness**, it is how long an overloaded or draining instance keeps getting new requests, and the\nmatching `successThreshold` decides how quickly it is trusted again. Choosing these from measured load\ntimes and latency, rather than copying defaults, is most of the difference between probes that help and\nprobes that cause outages.\n\n### Making checks cheap and honest\nProbes run often, so they must be fast and must not call the LLM. Deeper checks run on a background\nschedule and the endpoint returns the cached result, *because* a probe that waits on a slow dependency\ntimes out and reports failure for the wrong reason. Return a body explaining which condition failed, so\noperators following a runbook can see why an instance is unready.","example":"A FastAPI service that loads a model at startup, separates liveness from readiness and reports\noverload. The model loader is a stand-in for real initialisation. It runs as a background task because\nan `await` inside the lifespan would delay the server from accepting connections until loading ends:\nprobes would see connection refused rather than a 503 with a reason.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Response\n\nstate = {\"model\": None, \"in_flight\": 0}\nMAX_IN_FLIGHT = 32                       # illustrative per-replica concurrency budget\n\nasync def load_model():\n    await asyncio.sleep(5)               # stand-in; real blocking loads go in asyncio.to_thread\n    state[\"model\"] = object()\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app):\n    task = asyncio.create_task(load_model())   # load in background so probes answer meanwhile\n    yield\n    task.cancel()\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\n@app.get(\"/livez\")                      # restart only if the process itself is stuck\nasync def livez():\n    return {\"ok\": True}                 # reaching this handler proves the event loop runs\n\n@app.get(\"/startupz\")                   # gates liveness and readiness until the model is loaded\nasync def startupz(resp: Response):\n    if state[\"model\"] is None:\n        resp.status_code = 503\n    return {\"model_loaded\": state[\"model\"] is not None}\n\n@app.get(\"/readyz\")                     # route traffic here only if a request would succeed now\nasync def readyz(resp: Response):\n    reasons = []\n    if state[\"model\"] is None: reasons.append(\"model not loaded\")\n    if state[\"in_flight\"] >= MAX_IN_FLIGHT: reasons.append(\"at capacity\")\n    if reasons:\n        resp.status_code = 503\n    return {\"ready\": not reasons, \"reasons\": reasons}\n```\n\nNote that no check calls the LLM provider or the vector store. Their health is reported by metrics and a\nseparate synthetic check, so an upstream outage degrades the service instead of emptying the load\nbalancer. A real deployment increments and decrements `in_flight` around each generation (including\nthe whole duration of a streamed response) and configures draining in the platform, for example a\nKubernetes pre-stop delay, rather than overriding the server's own signal handling.","tradeoffs":["Deep readiness checks catch more broken instances, but couple every replica to shared dependencies; the balance flips toward deep checks only for dependencies that are per-instance, such as a local GPU.","Aggressive probe intervals and low failure thresholds detect problems fast, but cause flapping and needless restarts under load spikes; tolerate a few consecutive failures before acting.","Readiness based on load (\"at capacity\") sheds traffic gracefully, but if every replica hits capacity at once the service has no endpoints; pair it with autoscaling and a front-door rate limit.","Synthetic checks prove end-to-end correctness, but each one costs a real model call and can drift from real usage; keep them few, cheap and representative of the critical path."],"pitfalls":["Checking the LLM provider or database in the liveness probe; the symptom is every pod restarting at once during a short upstream incident, followed by slow cold starts.","No startup probe for a service that loads large weights; the symptom is pods killed by liveness during model loading and stuck in a restart loop.","Exiting without draining, so new requests still arrive during shutdown; the symptom is streamed responses cut off mid-answer on every deploy or scale-down.","Probes that are only shallow HTTP pings; the symptom is instances that report healthy while every request fails because the model never loaded or a GPU is in an error state.","Treating green probes as proof the product works; the symptom is all-green dashboards while users get wrong answers from a stale index, which only a synthetic end-to-end check would have caught."],"when":"Every deployed service needs liveness and readiness; any service with slow initialisation needs a startup\nprobe; any customer-facing AI system needs at least one synthetic end-to-end check on its critical path.\nDecide check scope by asking what action the check triggers: restart, route away, or alert. Watch probe\nfailure counts, restart counts, ready replica count and synthetic pass rate, and link each health alert\nto a runbook. In interviews, the expected depth is explaining why liveness must not include dependencies,\nhow readiness interacts with draining and rolling deploys, and why a probe cannot tell you answers are\ncorrect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements separate liveness, readiness and startup endpoints, keeps them fast and dependency-free, and writes the golden-request synthetic check with a meaningful assertion.","architect":"Decides which failures cause restarts, which cause routing changes and which only alert, so a shared dependency outage degrades the service rather than taking it down.","fde":"Adapts checks to the customer's load balancers and monitoring, which may only support one endpoint, and makes synthetic checks run inside their network against their real integrations."},"check":{"q":"After a 90-second outage at the LLM provider, the whole fleet restarted and the service was down\nfor twelve minutes. What went wrong in the health check design, and how would you change it?","a":"The provider was almost certainly checked by the liveness probe: readiness failures only remove\npods from endpoints and never restart them, so a fleet-wide restart points at liveness (a provider check\nin readiness would instead have emptied the endpoints for roughly the outage's duration). When the\nprovider failed, every replica failed at once; liveness restarted them all, and each restart\nmeant reloading model weights or warming caches, so recovery took far longer than the upstream outage.\nI would make liveness local only (process responsive), keep readiness to per-instance conditions such as\nmodel loaded, capacity and draining, and report provider health through metrics, alerts and a synthetic\ncheck. The request path should degrade with a fallback model or clear error, and add a startup probe so\nslow loads are not killed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens in Kubernetes when a pod fails its readiness probe but passes liveness?","options":["The container is restarted","The pod is removed from Service endpoints and stops receiving traffic, without a restart","The pod is rescheduled onto another node","Nothing, readiness is informational only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Readiness controls routing: an unready pod is taken out of the Service's endpoints until it passes again. Restarts are driven by liveness failures, and rescheduling is a scheduler decision, not a probe result."},{"q":"Why should a liveness probe avoid checking the external LLM provider?","options":["Provider calls are too expensive to run every few seconds","Liveness probes cannot make outbound network calls","The provider's own status page already reports its health","Restarting your instances cannot fix the provider, and simultaneous restarts turn a brief upstream issue into a fleet-wide outage"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cost is a real concern, but the decisive reason is the action liveness triggers. A restart only helps for failures inside the process; for shared dependencies it multiplies the damage."},{"q":"Which failure would a synthetic end-to-end check catch that liveness and readiness probes would not?","options":["A deadlocked worker thread","A pod that has not finished loading model weights","A retrieval index that stopped updating, so answers cite outdated documents","A pod that is shutting down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Probes check one instance's local condition. A stale index leaves every instance healthy while answers are wrong, which only a check asserting on real end-to-end output can detect."}]},{"key":"deployment/hypercare","sources":[],"why":"Go-live is the moment the system meets the full distribution of real work: every user, every document\ntype, every odd workflow and every integration under real load. UAT sampled that distribution; production\nis the distribution. For AI systems the gap is larger than for conventional software, because quality\ndepends on inputs the team has never seen. A retrieval system meets queries phrased in ways the golden set\ndid not anticipate; an extraction model meets the scanned fax from the one branch office that still uses\nthem; users discover behaviours nobody tested, such as pasting whole email threads into the assistant.\n\nAdoption is also fragile in this window. Users switching from a manual process form their opinion in the\nfirst days. If early failures are met with silence or a two-week support ticket queue, they route around\nthe system and return to the old way, and usage never recovers even after the bugs are fixed.\n\nThe naive alternative is to treat go-live as the finish line: hand over to the standard support process\nand move the team to the next project. Standard support is tuned for a stable system with known issues; it\ncannot tell a prompt regression from a user-training gap, and it has no route to change the prompt,\nretrieval or model. The opposite failure is open-ended hypercare, where the delivery team quietly becomes\nthe customer's permanent operations team. Hypercare exists to bridge the two: concentrated expertise for a\nbounded time, with a defined exit.","how":"### Plan it before go-live\nHypercare is agreed in the readiness review: its duration, staffing, hours of coverage, escalation paths,\nwhat counts as an incident versus a request, and the exit criteria. A duration of a few weeks is common,\nscaled to risk and user count; it is a planning choice, not a standard. Planning it in advance matters\n*because* the customer's support organisation, the vendor team and the sponsor all need to know who\nanswers what before the first problem arrives. The plan also fixes the mechanics: a single intake channel\nso issues are not scattered across email and chat, severity definitions with an agreed response time for\neach, coverage hours that match when users actually work (including other time zones), and who on each\nside is reachable out of hours for a severity-1.\n\n### Heightened monitoring\nDashboards and alert thresholds are tightened for the period, and someone actively watches them rather\nthan waiting for pages. For AI systems this includes signals that rarely page anyone: user feedback\nrates, escalation-to-human rate, guardrail blocks, retrieval misses (\"no relevant documents found\"),\ncost per task, and a daily sample of real conversations read by a person. Reading real transcripts is the\nfastest way to learn where the system fails, *because* metrics show that something changed while\ntranscripts show why.\n\n### A daily triage loop\nEach issue, ticket and piece of negative feedback is classified by cause: **defect** (system bug,\nintegration failure), **quality** (retrieval or model produced a poor answer), **knowledge gap** (the\nanswer is not in the indexed content), **training** (the user misunderstood what the system does), or\n**change request** (new scope). Classification decides the owner and the fix: engineers for defects,\nprompt or retrieval tuning for quality, the customer's content owners for knowledge gaps, enablement for\ntraining, the sponsor for change requests. A short daily review with the customer keeps both sides\nlooking at the same list.\n\n### Fast but safe fixes\nHypercare allows a faster release cadence, but every quality fix still goes through the regression suite,\nand every real failure becomes a new regression case, *because* a prompt change that fixes today's\ncomplaint can quietly break a case UAT had already accepted. Fixes ship through the same rollout criteria,\nwith shorter stages, not around them.\n\nThe complement of fast fixes is a freeze on everything else. Unrelated changes (a model upgrade, a new\nfeature, an index rebuild for another team) wait until hypercare ends, *because* the feedback signals are\nalready noisy and two changes shipped in the same week make it impossible to say which one moved the\nthumbs-down rate. Batching fixes into one or two predictable releases a week, each with a written note of\nwhat changed, keeps every movement in the metrics attributable.\n\n### Exit criteria and handoff\nHypercare ends when written criteria hold for a sustained period, for example: no open severity-1 or\nseverity-2 issues; incident and ticket volume stable or falling over consecutive weeks, normalised by\nactive users (because volume also falls when people stop using the system) and read alongside usage\nretention; quality metrics\nat or above the UAT baseline on real traffic; the customer's support team has resolved issues using the\nrunbooks without escalating; and the known-issues list and backlog are handed over with owners. The\nhandoff is explicit: a meeting, a signed-off document and a named date when the support model changes.","example":"A worked scenario: an HR policy assistant for a 4,000-employee company, three weeks of planned hypercare.\nExit criteria agreed at the readiness review: zero open sev-1/sev-2, weekly actionable issues below 10\nfor two consecutive weeks, thumbs-down rate at or below the 6% observed in UAT, and the customer's\nservice desk resolving at least 80% of tickets without vendor escalation.\n\n**Week 1.** 1,900 users, 41 actionable issues. Triage: 9 defects (most from single sign-on timeouts for\none region), 14 quality, 11 knowledge gaps (the parental-leave policy for one country was never in the\nindexed content), 5 training, 2 change requests. Thumbs-down rate 11%. The team fixes SSO, the customer's\nHR content owner uploads the missing policy, and the 14 quality failures become regression cases; a\nretrieval fix passes the suite and ships behind a flag.\n\n**Week 2.** 2,600 users, 18 issues, thumbs-down 7%, service desk resolves 65% unaided. One near miss: a\nprompt change improved answer tone but dropped citations on long answers; the regression suite caught it\nin staging, so it never shipped and never became a production incident.\n\n**Week 3.** 2,900 users, 9 issues, thumbs-down 5.8%, service desk at 78%. Per active user, issues fell\nfrom about 2.2 per 100 users in week 1 to about 0.3, while the user count kept rising, so the drop is\nreal improvement rather than people giving up. Volume is below 10 but only for\none week and desk resolution is below 80%, so exit criteria are not met. Rather than letting hypercare\ndrift on, the team extends it by one week with a single focus: a runbook session with the service desk\non the three ticket types they still escalate. Week 4 meets all criteria and support is handed over.","tradeoffs":["Longer hypercare catches more issues with experts on hand, but ties up the delivery team and delays the customer's own ownership; extend only against unmet exit criteria, never by default.","Embedding vendor engineers with the customer gives the fastest fixes, but can stop the customer team from learning; the balance flips once routine issues are being solved by vendor staff on request.","A faster release cadence during hypercare fixes real pain quickly, but raises regression risk; it is safe only when every change passes the regression suite and a shortened rollout.","Reading many real transcripts finds failure patterns early, but costs time and needs privacy approval; agree data-access rules for hypercare during the readiness review, not during an incident."],"pitfalls":["Starting hypercare with no exit criteria; the symptom is the delivery team still answering the customer's tickets months after go-live, with nobody able to say when it ends.","Fixing complaints with prompt tweaks that skip the regression suite; the symptom is a fix for this week's issue reopening a case accepted in UAT.","Lumping all feedback into \"bugs\"; the symptom is engineers spending days on problems that were missing content or user training, while real defects wait.","Measuring only incidents and uptime; the symptom is a quiet incident queue alongside falling usage, because unhappy users silently returned to the old process.","Doing the support work instead of transferring it; the symptom is a customer service desk that escalates every ticket on the first day after handoff."],"when":"Plan hypercare for any go-live where real users meet a new AI system, where the customer's own team will\neventually operate it, or where adoption matters commercially. Size it by risk and scale: a small\ninternal pilot may need a few days of close attention; an enterprise rollout to thousands of users needs\nweeks with dedicated staff. Track issue volume by cause, user feedback, usage and retention, quality on\nreal traffic versus the UAT baseline, and the share of tickets the customer resolves alone. In interviews,\nstrong answers describe hypercare as bounded, criteria-driven and aimed at transferring ownership, with\nAI-specific feedback classification.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns each real failure into a regression case, ships fixes through shortened rollouts, and builds the daily views of feedback, escalations and sampled transcripts used in triage.","architect":"Ensures the system supports hypercare safely: feature flags for quick fixes, regression gates that cannot be skipped, and data-access rules for reviewing real conversations.","fde":"Runs the daily triage with the customer, separates defects from content, training and scope issues, and drives the handoff so their team can operate the system without escalating."},"check":{"q":"Three weeks into hypercare, incidents are near zero but weekly active users have fallen 30% from\nweek one. The sponsor says hypercare can close. Do you agree?","a":"Not yet. A quiet incident queue with falling usage often means users stopped reporting because they\nstopped using the system. I would look at who dropped off and what they last did, read a sample of their\nfinal sessions, check feedback and abandonment by task type, and talk to a few users directly. Likely\ncauses are a quality gap on a common task, missing content, or a workflow that is slower than the old\nprocess. If the cause is fixable, extend hypercare against a specific adoption criterion; if it is a\ntraining or change-management issue, hand that to the customer's enablement owner explicitly. Closing on\nincident counts alone would hide the real failure."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should normally end a hypercare period?","options":["The planned number of weeks has passed","The delivery team is needed on another project","Written exit criteria have held for a sustained period and ownership is formally handed over","The first week with zero incidents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hypercare is bounded by criteria, not the calendar or staffing. A single quiet week can be noise or reflect falling usage, and calendar-based exits hand over a system the customer cannot yet run."},{"q":"Users report that the assistant cannot answer questions about one country's leave policy. The ingestion pipeline works, but the customer's HR team never supplied that policy for indexing. How should this be classified in triage?","options":["As a model quality defect needing prompt changes","As a user training issue","As a knowledge gap owned by the customer's content owners","As a change request to the sponsor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The system cannot answer from content it does not have. Tuning prompts would risk hallucinated answers; the right owner is whoever controls the source content, and the fix is to ingest the missing policy."},{"q":"Why must quality fixes during hypercare still pass the regression suite?","options":["Because the customer's change-management policy always requires it","Because a prompt or retrieval change that fixes one complaint can silently break cases already accepted in UAT","Because regression suites are the only way to measure latency","Because hypercare fixes are deployed to all users at once"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AI behaviour changes are global: a prompt edit affects every query. The regression suite, extended with each real failure, is what keeps a fast fix cadence from trading one problem for another."}]}]});
